// Question bank: original practice questions mapped to RIBO's 2025 Level 1 blueprint.
const Q=[
// GENERAL
{d:"gen",q:"A client insures a $30,000 car for its value, it is stolen, and the insurer pays $30,000. Which principle stops the client from also collecting the car's value from a second policy on the same car?",o:["Utmost good faith","Indemnity","Proximate cause","Insurable interest"],a:1,e:"Indemnity means insurance restores you to your pre-loss position and no further. Collecting twice would be a profit. When two policies cover the same loss, contribution splits it between them."},
{d:"gen",q:"For property insurance, when MUST the insured have an insurable interest?",o:["Only when the policy is applied for","At the time of loss","At both renewal and loss","Only when the claim is paid"],a:1,e:"For property, insurable interest must exist at the time of loss. If you sell the house before the fire, you've suffered no loss."},
{d:"gen",q:"After paying a client's claim for a fence knocked down by a neighbour's contractor, the insurer sues the contractor. This is an example of:",o:["Contribution","Subrogation","Indemnity","Salvage"],a:1,e:"Subrogation lets the insurer take over the insured's right to recover from the party responsible, once it has paid the claim."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which is a MORAL hazard?",o:["Oily rags stored next to a furnace","A home with knob-and-tube wiring","A struggling business owner who raises contents limits well above the stock's value","A house built on a flood plain"],a:2,e:"Moral hazard is about the insured's character or motives. The other three are physical hazards: tangible conditions that make a loss more likely."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer earned $20 million in premiums and incurred $14 million in claims. What is its loss ratio?",o:["43%","70%","143%","30%"],a:1,e:"Loss ratio = claims incurred ÷ premiums earned = 14 ÷ 20 = 70%."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which organization regulates insurance COMPANIES and approves auto insurance rates in Ontario?",o:["RIBO","FSRA","CCIR","IBAO"],a:1,e:"FSRA regulates insurers and agents and approves auto rates and underwriting rules. RIBO regulates brokers. CCIR is a national council of regulators. IBAO is a broker trade association."},
{d:"gen",q:"What is the main difference between a broker and a captive agent?",o:["Only brokers need a licence","A broker represents the client and can place business with several insurers; a captive agent represents one insurer","A captive agent can't sell auto insurance","A broker is employed by the insurer"],a:1,e:"Brokers act for the client and shop several markets. Captive agents sell for one insurer."},
{d:"gen",q:"The Facility Association exists to:",o:["Regulate brokers' trust accounts","Give auto insurance to drivers who can't get it in the regular market","Pay claims when an insurer goes bankrupt","Set the Fault Determination Rules"],a:1,e:"Facility Association is the auto residual market, funded by all auto insurers. Insurer insolvency is handled by a separate compensation body."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which best describes Lloyd's of London?",o:["A single British insurance company","A marketplace where syndicates of members underwrite risks","A reinsurance regulator","A Canadian mutual insurer"],a:1,e:"Lloyd's isn't an insurer. It's a market where syndicates accept risks, usually placed through specialized brokers or coverholders."},
{d:"gen",q:"A Level 1 licensee wants to open their own brokerage next month. Which is correct?",o:["They may, if they buy E&O insurance","They may not. A Level 1 licensee must work under the supervision of a Principal Broker at a registered brokerage","They may, only for personal lines","They may, after notifying FSRA"],a:1,e:"Level 1 is the 'acting under supervision' licence. Running a brokerage needs further licensing and experience."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client pays their annual premium to the brokerage by cheque. The brokerage MUST:",o:["Deposit it to the operating account and pay the insurer later","Deposit it to a trust account and use it only to pay the insurer or refund the client","Hold the cheque until the policy is issued","Deduct the commission and send the balance to RIBO"],a:1,e:"Premiums are trust money under RIBO's regulations. They can't be used for the brokerage's operating expenses."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which change MUST a broker register with RIBO?",o:["A salary increase","Moving to a different brokerage","Taking a CE course","Starting to quote condo policies"],a:1,e:"Changes to registration details, such as your employer brokerage, must be reported under RIBO's by-laws. Pay and product mix are not registration information."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker tells a client they'll arrange a mortgage discount only if the client also moves their home and auto insurance to the brokerage. This is:",o:["Cross-selling, which is encouraged","Tied selling, which is prohibited","Acceptable if disclosed in writing","Allowed if the client saves money"],a:1,e:"Making one product a condition of getting another is tied selling, which is prohibited. Offering a bundle discount is fine. Forcing a purchase is not."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker's brokerage is partly owned by one of the insurers it places business with. When must the broker tell the client?",o:["Only if the client asks","Before making a recommendation","At the first claim","Never, because ownership is public record"],a:1,e:"Conflicts of interest, including ownership and compensation arrangements, must be disclosed before you recommend a product."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer adds a new identity-theft endorsement to every renewal and charges for it unless the client calls to remove it. This practice is called:",o:["Automatic renewal","Negative option billing","Pro-rata billing","Short-rate cancellation"],a:1,e:"Charging for a product the client didn't agree to, unless they opt out, is negative option billing. It's prohibited."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client gave their email for an auto quote. The brokerage now wants to email them a promotion for pet insurance. Under CASL the brokerage:",o:["Can email freely because they're a client","Needs the client's consent to send commercial electronic messages","Can only send it by text","Must get RIBO's approval"],a:1,e:"CASL requires consent (express or, in limited cases, implied) before sending commercial electronic messages, plus a working unsubscribe."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client asks for sewer backup coverage. The broker forgets to add it, and the basement floods from a sewer backup the next month. Which coverage protects the BROKER?",o:["The client's homeowners policy","Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance","Commercial general liability","Crime insurance"],a:1,e:"E&O covers a broker's professional negligence, such as failing to place coverage the client asked for."},
{d:"gen",q:"An applicant doesn't mention three recent at-fault accidents. After a large claim, the insurer finds out. The likely result is:",o:["The claim is paid, then the premium is adjusted","The insurer may void the policy or deny the claim for material misrepresentation","The broker must pay the claim","Nothing, because the applicant wasn't asked in writing"],a:1,e:"Utmost good faith requires disclosing material facts. Material misrepresentation lets the insurer void the contract or deny the claim, subject to the policy and law."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker has 'binding authority' with an insurer. This means the broker can:",o:["Settle claims up to $10,000","Put coverage in force for the insurer, within the limits the insurer has set","Change policy wordings","Set the premium rates"],a:1,e:"Binding authority is a contractual power to commit the insurer to coverage immediately, within agreed limits and classes."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which province's private law is based on the Civil Code rather than common law?",o:["Ontario","British Columbia","Quebec","Nova Scotia"],a:2,e:"Quebec uses the Civil Code for private law. The rest of Canada uses common law."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client calls the broker right after a kitchen fire. The broker's BEST first action is to:",o:["Tell the client it's definitely covered","Report the loss to the insurer promptly and explain the claims process","Tell the client to call a lawyer","Wait for the client to send photos before doing anything"],a:1,e:"The broker should give first notice of loss quickly and manage the client's expectations. Only the insurer or adjuster decides coverage."},
// AUTO
{d:"auto",q:"What is the minimum third-party liability limit required on an Ontario private passenger auto policy?",o:["$100,000","$200,000","$500,000","$1,000,000"],a:1,e:"The legal minimum is $200,000. Most brokers recommend $1M–$2M because injury lawsuits easily exceed the minimum."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is 25% at fault under the Fault Determination Rules for a collision in Ontario with another insured car. The damage to their car is $8,000, and they have DCPD with no deductible. How much does DCPD pay?",o:["$8,000","$6,000","$2,000","$0, because they were partly at fault"],a:1,e:"DCPD pays to the extent you are not at fault: $8,000 × 75% = $6,000. The remaining $2,000 is paid only under Collision or All Perils, after that deductible."},
{d:"auto",q:"Which endorsement lets a client opt out of DCPD coverage?",o:["OPCF 20","OPCF 44R","OPCF 49","OPCF 28A"],a:2,e:"OPCF 49, the Agreement Not to Recover, removes DCPD. The client then gets no payment for their own car damage under Section 6, even when not at fault."},
{d:"auto",q:"Car A rear-ends Car B, which was travelling in the same lane and direction. Under the Fault Determination Rules:",o:["Each driver is 50% at fault","Car A is 100% at fault, Car B 0%","Car B is 25% at fault for braking","Fault is decided by police"],a:1,e:"In a same-lane, same-direction rear-end collision, the following car is 100% at fault. The rules apply regardless of road or weather conditions."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is badly hurt by an at-fault driver who has only the $200,000 minimum liability. Your client has a $2,000,000 liability limit. Which endorsement could make up the difference?",o:["OPCF 27","OPCF 44R","OPCF 43","OPCF 5"],a:1,e:"OPCF 44R Family Protection covers the gap when the at-fault driver's limit is too low, up to your own liability limit."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client wants a rental car while theirs is in the shop after a covered collision. Which endorsement provides this?",o:["OPCF 16","OPCF 20","OPCF 23A","OPCF 40"],a:1,e:"OPCF 20 is loss of use / transportation replacement. It pays for a rental after a covered loss, up to its limits."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client often rents cars on business trips in Canada and the U.S. and wants protection for damage to the rentals. Recommend:",o:["OPCF 27","OPCF 28A","OPCF 19","OPCF 49"],a:0,e:"OPCF 27 covers liability for damage to non-owned automobiles, such as rental cars and borrowed cars."},
{d:"auto",q:"A family wants to keep a high-risk teenager from driving the family car so their premium drops. Which endorsement excludes the named driver from all coverage?",o:["OPCF 28A","OPCF 44R","OPCF 39","OPCF 17"],a:0,e:"OPCF 28A excludes a named driver. If that person drives the car anyway, there is no coverage for the loss. Explain this clearly and document it."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client buys a brand-new car and wants it replaced with a new one, not paid at depreciated value, if it's written off in the first couple of years. Recommend:",o:["OPCF 43","OPCF 20","OPCF 40","OPCF 5"],a:0,e:"OPCF 43 (and 43A for leased vehicles) removes the depreciation deduction for a set period."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client finances their car through a bank, which requires its interest to be protected on the policy. Which endorsement applies?",o:["OPCF 23A","OPCF 16","OPCF 2","OPCF 49"],a:0,e:"OPCF 23A is the lienholder (mortgage) clause. It protects the lender's interest in physical damage payments."},
{d:"auto",q:"Hail dents a client's parked car. Which coverage pays?",o:["Collision/Upset","Comprehensive (or All Perils)","DCPD","Third-party liability"],a:1,e:"Hail is not a collision. Comprehensive covers it, as does All Perils, which combines Collision and Comprehensive. Specified Perils lists hail too."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client stores their convertible from November to April and wants to stop paying for driving coverage. Which endorsements are used?",o:["OPCF 16 to suspend, OPCF 17 to reinstate","OPCF 28 and 28A","OPCF 19 and 19A","OPCF 39 and 40"],a:0,e:"OPCF 16 suspends coverage (usually keeping comprehensive while stored), and OPCF 17 reinstates it."},
{d:"auto",q:"An insurer wants to cancel an auto policy and sends notice by registered mail. How much notice is required under the statutory conditions?",o:["5 days","10 days","15 days","30 days"],a:2,e:"The insurer may terminate with 15 days' notice by registered mail or 5 days' notice delivered personally."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is hit by a driver who flees and is never identified. Which OAP 1 section helps with your client's injuries?",o:["Section 3, Third Party Liability","Section 5, Uninsured Automobile","Section 6, DCPD","Section 7, Collision"],a:1,e:"Uninsured Automobile coverage responds to injury caused by uninsured or unidentified drivers."},
{d:"auto",q:"Under the accident benefit rules in force from July 1, 2026, which benefits remain MANDATORY on every Ontario auto policy?",o:["Income replacement and death benefits","Medical, rehabilitation and attendant care","Caregiver and housekeeping","All benefits remain mandatory"],a:1,e:"Since July 1, 2026, only medical, rehabilitation and attendant care are mandatory. The others, including income replacement and death and funeral, are optional.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"For a non-catastrophic injury, what is the combined standard limit for medical, rehabilitation and attendant care benefits?",o:["$3,500","$36,000","$65,000","$1,000,000"],a:2,e:"$65,000 combined for non-catastrophic injuries. Catastrophic injuries have a $1,000,000 combined limit. Check the latest FSRA summary before your exam.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"Which law requires every motor vehicle driven on an Ontario highway to be insured?",o:["Insurance Act","Highway Traffic Act","Compulsory Automobile Insurance Act","Motor Vehicle Accident Claims Act"],a:2,e:"The Compulsory Automobile Insurance Act makes insurance mandatory and sets heavy fines for driving uninsured."},
{d:"auto",q:"An injured pedestrian has no auto insurance of their own and was hit by an uninsured driver. Which source pays as a last resort?",o:["Facility Association","Motor Vehicle Accident Claims Fund","OHIP","RIBO"],a:1,e:"The Motor Vehicle Accident Claims Fund pays victims when there is no insurance to respond."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client starts using their personal car for paid food delivery three evenings a week. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing, because personal use includes part-time work","Treat it as a material change, report it to the insurer and arrange coverage for delivery use","Tell the client to keep it quiet","Cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"Delivery for pay is a change in use. Personal policies typically exclude it unless the insurer adds coverage or the delivery platform's policy responds."},
{d:"auto",q:"Where does the OAP 1 provide coverage?",o:["Ontario only","Canada only","Canada, the United States, and on vessels between ports of the two","Anywhere in the world"],a:2,e:"The OAP 1 territory is Canada, the U.S., and vessels travelling between ports of those countries."},
{d:"auto",q:"The 'Take All Comers' rule means an Ontario auto insurer:",o:["Must insure every applicant at the same rate","Must offer coverage to any applicant who meets its approved underwriting rules","Can't cancel any policy","Must accept drivers with suspended licences"],a:1,e:"Insurers can decline people who don't meet their filed underwriting rules, but must offer coverage to everyone who does."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car is damaged in a collision with an insured car in Ontario. They were 0% at fault. Under which section does their own insurer pay for the car damage?",o:["Section 3","Section 5","Section 6, DCPD","Section 7, Collision"],a:2,e:"DCPD (Section 6) pays your own vehicle damage to the extent you're not at fault, if the collision meets its conditions. Section 7 is for the at-fault share or when DCPD doesn't apply."},
// HABITATIONAL
{d:"hab",q:"A windstorm destroys a homeowner's detached garden shed. Which coverage applies?",o:["Coverage A, Dwelling","Coverage B, Detached Private Structures","Coverage C, Personal Property","Coverage D, Additional Living Expense"],a:1,e:"Detached structures such as sheds, detached garages and fences fall under Coverage B."},
{d:"hab",q:"A fire makes a family's home unlivable for three months, and they rent an apartment. Which coverage pays the EXTRA cost of living elsewhere?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage C","Coverage D","Coverage E"],a:2,e:"Coverage D, additional living expense, pays the increase in living costs while the home is being repaired."},
{d:"hab",q:"A guest slips on the insured's stairs. The insured wants a small physiotherapy bill paid even though they may not be legally liable. Which coverage pays it?",o:["Coverage E, Legal Liability","Coverage F, Voluntary Medical Payments","Coverage G, Voluntary Property Damage","Coverage H, Residence Employees"],a:1,e:"Coverage F pays reasonable medical expenses of others regardless of legal liability. It helps keep small incidents out of court."},
{d:"hab",q:"A 'broad form' homeowners policy typically insures:",o:["Building and contents on a named perils basis","Building on an all risks basis and contents on a named perils basis","Building and contents on an all risks basis","Contents only"],a:1,e:"Basic = named perils for both. Broad = all risks building, named perils contents. Comprehensive = all risks for both."},
{d:"hab",q:"A home worth $400,000 has an 80% co-insurance clause and is insured for $240,000. A $100,000 loss occurs. Ignoring the deductible, what does the insurer pay?",o:["$60,000","$75,000","$80,000","$100,000"],a:1,e:"Required = 80% × $400,000 = $320,000. Payment = 240 ÷ 320 × $100,000 = $75,000."},
{d:"hab",q:"Heavy rain causes the municipal sewer to back up into a client's basement. They have a standard comprehensive homeowners policy with no water endorsements. The loss is:",o:["Covered, because comprehensive covers all risks","Not covered without a sewer backup endorsement","Covered under Coverage D only","Covered only if the house is less than 10 years old"],a:1,e:"Sewer backup is excluded by standard wordings and needs a specific endorsement, even on comprehensive forms."},
{d:"hab",q:"A river overflows and floods a client's home at ground level. Which endorsement would have been needed?",o:["Sewer backup","Overland water","Identity theft","Home-based business"],a:1,e:"Overland water covers water flowing over the ground surface from rivers, lakes or heavy rainfall. It's separate from sewer backup."},
{d:"hab",q:"Why do brokers recommend buying overland water, sewer backup and ground water coverage together?",o:["Insurers require all three by law","One water event often involves several causes, and a missing cause can reduce or defeat the claim","It is always cheaper per endorsement","RIBO prohibits selling them separately"],a:1,e:"Concurrent causation: storms often cause overland flow and sewer backup at once. Wordings can exclude a loss when an excluded cause contributes."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner leaves on a 6-week trip and nobody stays in or checks the house. Which statement is MOST accurate?",o:["Coverage is unchanged","Some perils, such as vandalism, glass breakage and water damage, may be suspended once the home is vacant beyond the policy's limit (often 30 days), and freezing losses need the policy's conditions met","The whole policy is cancelled automatically","Only theft is excluded"],a:1,e:"Vacancy provisions suspend some coverage, and freezing exclusions apply unless someone checks the home as required or the water is shut off and drained. Tell clients before long absences."},
{d:"hab",q:"A tenant asks if their tenant's policy will pay to rebuild the apartment building after a fire. The correct answer is:",o:["Yes, under Coverage A","No. The building is the landlord's to insure. The tenant's policy covers their contents, extra living costs, and liability, including damage they cause to the landlord's property","Yes, if the tenant caused the fire","Only if the landlord has no insurance"],a:1,e:"Tenant policies have no building coverage. Their liability coverage responds if the tenant is legally liable for damage to the landlord's property."},
{d:"hab",q:"After a large loss to the condo building, the condo corporation charges each owner a share of its $50,000 deductible. Which unit owner coverage responds?",o:["Improvements and betterments","Loss assessment / deductible assessment","Additional living expense","Coverage F"],a:1,e:"Loss assessment coverage pays the owner's share of assessments, including the corporation's deductible, up to the limit."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's $15,000 engagement ring is stolen. Their homeowners policy has a special limit for jewellery theft. To avoid this problem, the broker should have:",o:["Raised Coverage C","Scheduled the ring for its appraised value","Added overland water","Lowered the deductible"],a:1,e:"Special limits cap theft of jewellery and similar items. Scheduling insures the item for its full appraised value, usually with broader coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client starts running a hair salon from their basement with two customers a day. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing, because a small business is covered","Report the change and arrange a home-based business endorsement or separate business policy","Increase Coverage A","Tell the client to stop"],a:1,e:"Homeowners policies limit or exclude business property and business liability. A home-based business is a material change."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client begins renting their whole house on a short-term rental app on weekends. This is:",o:["Not relevant to their insurance","A material change that must be disclosed. It may need a home-sharing endorsement or a different policy","Covered under Coverage D","Covered because guests are invitees"],a:1,e:"Short-term renting changes the risk. Undisclosed, it can lead to a denied claim. Many insurers offer home-sharing endorsements."},
{d:"hab",q:"A TV cost $1,500 to replace new today and has depreciated 60%. On an actual cash value basis, the insurer pays (ignoring deductible):",o:["$1,500","$900","$600","$0"],a:2,e:"ACV = replacement cost − depreciation = $1,500 − $900 = $600."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client buys a 150 hp powerboat. Why should the broker look beyond the homeowners policy?",o:["Homeowners policies never cover boats","Homeowners policies limit watercraft coverage by size or horsepower, so a large boat needs a watercraft policy or endorsement","Boats are covered only under auto policies","RIBO forbids insuring boats on homeowners"],a:1,e:"Homeowners wordings cap watercraft property and liability by horsepower or length. Larger boats need their own coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"The furnace fails while a family is away in January and pipes freeze and burst. Under most homeowners wordings, the loss is covered:",o:["Always","Never","Only if the policy's conditions were met, for example someone checking the home as required or the water shut off and drained","Only if the furnace was new"],a:2,e:"Freezing exclusions apply while the home is unheated unless the insured took the precautions the wording requires."},
{d:"hab",q:"Which coverage pays expenses to restore a client's credit after their identity is stolen?",o:["Coverage E","An identity theft (cyber) endorsement","Coverage B","Overland water"],a:1,e:"Identity theft endorsements pay costs such as lost wages, legal fees and document replacement to restore identity."},
// COMMERCIAL
{d:"com",q:"A CGL policy written on an OCCURRENCE form responds when:",o:["The claim is made during the policy period","The injury or damage happens during the policy period, even if the claim comes years later","The insured reports it within 30 days","The policy has a retroactive date"],a:1,e:"Occurrence forms are triggered by when the injury or damage happens. Claims-made forms are triggered by when the claim is made."},
{d:"com",q:"Professional liability policies for accountants are usually written on which basis?",o:["Occurrence","Claims-made","Named perils","Valued"],a:1,e:"Professional and E&O policies are usually claims-made, often with a retroactive date. Keeping coverage continuous matters."},
{d:"com",q:"A restaurant is closed for two months after a covered fire. Which coverage replaces its lost profits and pays continuing expenses such as rent and key staff wages?",o:["Equipment breakdown","Business interruption","Builders' risk","Crime"],a:1,e:"Business interruption pays lost income and continuing expenses after an insured property loss stops operations."},
{d:"com",q:"A medical clinic must reopen right away in temporary premises after a fire, at higher cost. Which coverage fits?",o:["Extra expense","Tenants' legal liability","Fidelity","Surety bond"],a:0,e:"Extra expense pays the additional cost of continuing operations, for businesses that can't afford to close."},
{d:"com",q:"A sole proprietor is sued for $2 million and the business has no insurance. Who is at risk?",o:["Only the business assets","The owner personally, including personal assets","Nobody, because businesses can't be sued","The provincial government"],a:1,e:"A sole proprietorship isn't a separate legal entity, so the owner is personally liable. A corporation is a separate legal entity."},
{d:"com",q:"Three insurers each take a share (50%, 30%, 20%) of a large warehouse risk under one policy. This is a:",o:["Blanket policy","Subscription policy","Reinsurance treaty","Facility Association placement"],a:1,e:"In a subscription policy, each insurer is liable only for its own percentage share."},
{d:"com",q:"A factory's main compressor suffers a sudden internal electrical breakdown. The standard property form excludes this. Which coverage responds?",o:["Equipment breakdown (boiler and machinery)","Crime","CGL","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Equipment breakdown covers sudden mechanical or electrical breakdown, which property forms exclude."},
{d:"com",q:"An employee steals $40,000 from a business over a year by creating fake suppliers. Which coverage applies?",o:["CGL","Employee dishonesty (crime)","Business interruption","D&O"],a:1,e:"Employee dishonesty covers theft by employees. CGL doesn't cover the insured's own financial loss from theft."},
{d:"com",q:"A contractor is building a new house for a client. Which policy covers the structure while it's under construction?",o:["Homeowners","Builders' risk","Tenants' legal liability","Garage policy"],a:1,e:"Builders' risk covers buildings and materials during construction."},
{d:"com",q:"An auto repair shop wants coverage for customers' cars in its care and its garage operations. Which policy form is used?",o:["OAP 1","OAP 4 (garage)","SPF 6","Homeowners"],a:1,e:"The OAP 4 garage policy covers auto service businesses and vehicles in their care, custody or control."},
{d:"com",q:"Employees use their own cars to visit clients. The employer could be sued after an employee's accident. What protects the employer?",o:["OPCF 28A","Non-owned automobile liability (SPF 6)","OAP 4","DCPD"],a:1,e:"SPF 6 covers the business's liability from vehicles it doesn't own being used for its business."},
{d:"com",q:"A landlord asks a tenant business for a certificate of insurance naming the landlord. The certificate:",o:["Amends the policy to add the landlord","Is evidence that coverage exists. It doesn't change or extend the policy","Guarantees coverage for any claim","Replaces the policy"],a:1,e:"Certificates only show coverage. Adding someone as an additional insured needs an actual policy endorsement."},
{d:"com",q:"A commercial building worth $1,000,000 has a 90% co-insurance clause and is insured for $900,000. There is a $50,000 loss. Ignoring the deductible, the insurer pays:",o:["$45,000","$50,000","$40,500","$90,000"],a:1,e:"Required = 90% × $1,000,000 = $900,000. The insured carries the full requirement, so the full $50,000 is paid."},
{d:"com",q:"Shareholders sue a company's directors, alleging poor decisions that destroyed share value. Which policy is designed for this?",o:["CGL","D&O (directors and officers)","E&O","Crime"],a:1,e:"D&O covers directors' and officers' wrongful acts in managing the organization."},
{d:"com",q:"A surety bond involves three parties. Which group is correct?",o:["Insured, insurer, broker","Principal, obligee, surety","Lessor, lessee, lender","Policyholder, adjuster, regulator"],a:1,e:"The surety guarantees to the obligee that the principal will perform. If the surety pays, it can recover from the principal."},
{d:"com",q:"In Ontario, which vehicle generally requires the operator to hold a CVOR certificate?",o:["A private passenger SUV","A commercial truck with a registered gross weight over 4,500 kg","Any car used for business","A motorcycle"],a:1,e:"CVOR (Commercial Vehicle Operator's Registration) applies to trucks over 4,500 kg and buses, among others."},
{d:"com",q:"A tenant business carelessly starts a fire that damages the landlord's building. Which CGL coverage responds?",o:["Coverage B, Personal and Advertising Injury","Coverage C, Medical Payments","Coverage D, Tenants' Legal Liability","Business interruption"],a:2,e:"Tenants' legal liability covers damage to premises rented by the insured that it is legally liable for."},
// TRAVEL
{d:"trv",q:"Why should an Ontario resident buy travel medical insurance for a U.S. trip?",o:["OHIP doesn't cover anything outside Ontario","OHIP pays only a small part of emergency costs outside Canada","It is required by law","It replaces auto insurance in the U.S."],a:1,e:"OHIP covers only a small portion of out-of-country emergency costs. U.S. hospital bills can be huge."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client's blood pressure medication dose was changed three weeks before departure. Their policy requires conditions to be stable for 90 days. What is the risk?",o:["None, because dose changes don't count","The condition may be considered unstable, so related claims could be excluded. The client should disclose it and review coverage options","The whole trip is uninsurable","Only baggage coverage is affected"],a:1,e:"Medication changes usually count against stability clauses. Accurate disclosure on the medical questionnaire is essential."},
{d:"trv",q:"A traveller is admitted to hospital abroad. Under most travel medical policies, what should happen first?",o:["Pay and claim when home","Contact the insurer's assistance company as soon as possible","Call the broker's office the next business day","Contact OHIP"],a:1,e:"Most policies require prompt contact with the assistance company, and payments can be limited if you don't call."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client cancels a prepaid trip before departure because their parent became seriously ill. Which coverage pays the non-refundable costs?",o:["Emergency medical","Trip cancellation","Baggage","Flight accident"],a:1,e:"Trip cancellation reimburses non-refundable prepaid costs when the trip is cancelled before departure for a covered reason."},
{d:"trv",q:"The government issues an 'avoid non-essential travel' advisory for a country BEFORE a client leaves, and they go anyway. Claims related to that advisory are:",o:["Always covered","Often excluded","Covered at double the limit","Paid by the government"],a:1,e:"Most policies exclude claims linked to an advisory that was in place before departure. Check the wording."},
// ===== SET 2 =====
// GENERAL
{d:"gen",q:"Which principle looks at the dominant, effective cause of a loss to decide whether it is covered?",o:["Contribution","Proximate cause","Subrogation","Utmost good faith"],a:1,e:"Proximate cause is the dominant, effective cause that sets the chain of events in motion. Coverage depends on whether that cause is insured."},
{d:"gen",q:"A policy wording is genuinely ambiguous, and the insurer and insured read it differently. Courts usually:",o:["Read it in favour of the insurer, because the insurer wrote it","Read it against the insurer, because the insurer drafted the contract","Void the policy","Ask FSRA to decide"],a:1,e:"An insurance policy is a contract of adhesion: the insured can't negotiate the wording. Courts interpret real ambiguity against the drafter (contra proferentem)."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which is NOT an essential element of a valid insurance contract?",o:["Offer and acceptance","Consideration (the premium)","Legal capacity of the parties","A witness's signature"],a:3,e:"A valid contract needs offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity and a legal purpose. A witness isn't required."},
{d:"gen",q:"What is the MAIN purpose of a deductible?",o:["To raise the insurer's profit","To remove small claims and lower the premium by having the insured share the loss","To punish insureds who claim","To meet co-insurance"],a:1,e:"Deductibles cut the cost of handling small claims and give insureds a reason to prevent losses, which keeps premiums lower."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer transfers part of a large risk it has written to another company. This is:",o:["Subrogation","Reinsurance","Co-insurance","Binding"],a:1,e:"Reinsurance is insurance for insurers. It spreads large or catastrophe exposures."},
{d:"gen",q:"Whose job is to evaluate a risk and decide whether to accept it and on what terms?",o:["Claims adjuster","Underwriter","Broker","Appraiser"],a:1,e:"Underwriters accept, decline and price risks. Adjusters investigate and settle claims. Brokers advise clients and place the business."},
{d:"gen",q:"After a large fire, a homeowner hires a professional to prepare and negotiate their claim with the insurer, for a fee. This person is a:",o:["Staff adjuster","Independent adjuster","Public adjuster","Underwriter"],a:2,e:"A public adjuster works for the insured. Staff and independent adjusters work for the insurer."},
{d:"gen",q:"Who owns a mutual insurance company?",o:["Its shareholders","Its policyholders","The province","Its brokers"],a:1,e:"Mutuals are owned by their policyholders. Stock companies are owned by shareholders."},
{d:"gen",q:"To prove negligence, a claimant must show duty of care, breach of that duty, and:",o:["Intent to harm","Causation and resulting damage","A police report","A written contract"],a:1,e:"Negligence requires a duty of care, a breach, causation and actual damage. Intent isn't required."},
{d:"gen",q:"A delivery employee injures a pedestrian while making deliveries. The employer can be held liable even though it did nothing wrong itself. This is:",o:["Contractual liability","Vicarious liability","Absolute liability","Strict product liability"],a:1,e:"Vicarious liability makes an employer responsible for employees' acts in the course of employment."},
{d:"gen",q:"A 'material fact' on an insurance application is one that:",o:["The client considers important","Would influence the insurer's decision to accept the risk or set the premium","Appears on the declarations page","Is required by RIBO"],a:1,e:"Materiality is judged by its effect on the insurer's decision to accept or rate the risk."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker accidentally emails a client's driver's licence and claims history to the wrong person. The broker should FIRST:",o:["Ignore it if the recipient seems trustworthy","Follow the brokerage's privacy breach procedure and tell the privacy officer","Delete the sent email and move on","Call the client's insurer to cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"Report it internally right away. Under PIPEDA, breaches that create a real risk of significant harm must be reported to the Privacy Commissioner and to the affected people."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client complains that their insurer unfairly denied a claim, and the insurer's internal complaint process has ended. Where can the client go next?",o:["RIBO","The General Insurance OmbudService","IBAO","Facility Association"],a:1,e:"The General Insurance OmbudService handles consumer disputes with insurers once internal complaint processes are finished. RIBO handles complaints about brokers."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client complains that a broker ignored their instructions and was rude. Which body handles complaints about broker conduct?",o:["FSRA","RIBO","General Insurance OmbudService","CCIR"],a:1,e:"RIBO regulates broker conduct and runs the complaints and discipline process for brokers."},
{d:"gen",q:"An email that looks like it's from an insurer's portal asks the broker to 'verify your password' through a link. The broker should:",o:["Click the link and log in quickly","Not click it and report it as suspected phishing under brokerage procedures","Forward it to clients as a warning","Reply with the password"],a:1,e:"This is a classic phishing attempt. Brokers must follow cyber security procedures to protect client data."},
{d:"gen",q:"Why have many insurers raised prices or restricted water coverage in certain neighbourhoods?",o:["RIBO requires it","More frequent and severe water losses, including climate-related events","Lower building costs","The Take All Comers rule"],a:1,e:"Climate change and severe weather have increased water losses. Insurers price and underwrite by location risk."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which designation is aimed specifically at insurance brokers?",o:["CAIB","CPA","CFP","P.Eng"],a:0,e:"CAIB (Canadian Accredited Insurance Broker) is the broker designation. CIP and FCIP come from the Insurance Institute and cover the wider industry."},
{d:"gen",q:"Law that develops through court decisions and precedent is called:",o:["Statute law","Common law","Civil Code","Regulation"],a:1,e:"Common law grows from judges' decisions. Statutes are passed by legislatures, and regulations are made under statutes."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which principle applies throughout the whole client relationship, from advertising to claims, under CCIR and CISRO guidance?",o:["Take All Comers","Fair Treatment of Customers","Indemnity","Contribution"],a:1,e:"Fair Treatment of Customers expects insurers and intermediaries to treat customers fairly at every stage of the product life cycle."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client renews by phone and asks the broker to process it. Using the client's information to process that renewal is an example of:",o:["No consent","Implied consent for a purpose the client would reasonably expect","Express written consent only","A privacy breach"],a:1,e:"Consent can be implied when the use is obvious and expected. New, unexpected uses, such as marketing, need fresh consent."},
// AUTO
{d:"auto",q:"A client's 19-year-old son lives at home, has a licence, and occasionally drives the family car. What must the client do?",o:["Nothing, because he only drives occasionally","Disclose him as a driver on the policy","Add OPCF 27","Buy him a separate policy"],a:1,e:"All licensed drivers in the household and regular drivers must be disclosed. Leaving one out can be a material misrepresentation."},
{d:"auto",q:"Under Ontario's Highway Traffic Act, when must a collision be reported to police or a collision reporting centre?",o:["Only if someone is injured","When anyone is injured or combined damage appears to exceed $2,000","Only if police attend","Only for at-fault drivers"],a:1,e:"The reporting threshold is any injury, or apparent damage of more than $2,000. Brokers should know this when helping clients after an accident."},
{d:"auto",q:"Within how many days of a loss to the insured car must a proof of loss generally be filed under the OAP 1 statutory conditions?",o:["7 days","30 days","90 days","1 year"],a:2,e:"Proof of loss for damage to the car is due within 90 days. Notice of the loss itself must be given promptly."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client with a whiplash-type sprain is treated under the Minor Injury Guideline. What is the standard limit for medical and rehab under that guideline?",o:["$2,000","$3,500","$65,000","$1,000,000"],a:1,e:"Treatment for minor injuries (sprains, strains, whiplash) is capped at $3,500 under the Minor Injury Guideline.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"For a catastrophic impairment, what is the standard combined limit for medical, rehabilitation and attendant care benefits?",o:["$65,000","$500,000","$1,000,000","$2,000,000"],a:2,e:"$1,000,000 combined for catastrophic impairments. Higher limits can be bought as optional coverage.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"If a client buys income replacement benefits under the post-July 2026 rules, the standard benefit is 70% of gross income up to:",o:["$400 a week","$600 a week","$1,000 a week","$2,000 a week"],a:0,e:"The standard amount is 70% of gross income to a maximum of $400 a week. Higher weekly limits are available as options. Since July 1, 2026, income replacement itself is optional.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"Since July 1, 2026, optional accident benefits on a policy apply to which people?",o:["Anyone injured by the insured car","The named insured, spouse, dependants and listed drivers","Only the named insured","Only passengers"],a:1,e:"FSRA's guidance limits optional benefits to the named insured, their spouse, dependants and listed drivers. Others rely on the mandatory benefits.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's parked car is struck and damaged by a driver who leaves and is never identified. Which coverage pays to repair the car?",o:["Section 5, Uninsured Automobile","Collision/Upset or All Perils","Third-party liability","DCPD"],a:1,e:"Uninsured auto pays car damage only when the uninsured driver is identified. Damage by an unidentified driver is a Collision or All Perils claim."},
{d:"auto",q:"Which loss is NOT covered under Specified Perils?",o:["Fire","Theft","Hail","Vandalism"],a:3,e:"Specified Perils lists perils such as fire, theft, lightning, windstorm, hail and rising water. Vandalism needs Comprehensive or All Perils."},
{d:"auto",q:"A stone from a truck cracks a client's windshield on the highway. The client has Collision and Comprehensive. Which coverage pays?",o:["Collision","Comprehensive","DCPD","Third-party liability"],a:1,e:"Glass damage from flying objects is a Comprehensive loss."},
{d:"auto",q:"An Ontario client drives in a U.S. state that requires a higher minimum liability limit than their policy has. The OAP 1:",o:["Gives no coverage in that state","Provides at least the minimum limit required in that state","Pays only $200,000","Requires an OPCF 27"],a:1,e:"The OAP 1 automatically meets the minimum liability limits required where the accident happens in Canada or the U.S."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client wants to rent their personal car to strangers through a peer-to-peer app. What does the broker tell them?",o:["It's covered as personal use","Renting the car out isn't covered unless the insurer approves it (for example with an OPCF 5) or the platform's insurance applies. Disclose it first.","OPCF 44R covers it","DCPD covers it"],a:1,e:"Renting the car out changes the risk and is excluded without permission. OPCF 5 grants permission to rent or lease."},
{d:"auto",q:"What does an OPCF 39 (accident waiver) do?",o:["Waives the deductible for every claim","Stops the client's first at-fault accident from affecting their premium","Removes DCPD","Waives accident benefits"],a:1,e:"Accident waiver protects the premium from the first at-fault accident, under the insurer's eligibility rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"Car A changes lanes and collides with Car B, which is travelling straight in its own lane. Under the Fault Determination Rules:",o:["50/50","Car A is 100% at fault","Car B is 100% at fault","Fault depends on speed"],a:1,e:"The vehicle changing lanes is 100% at fault for a collision with a vehicle already in that lane."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's legally parked car is hit by a moving car. The client's fault share is:",o:["0%","25%","50%","Decided by police"],a:0,e:"A properly parked car is 0% at fault. The moving vehicle is 100% at fault."},
{d:"auto",q:"At an intersection controlled by traffic lights, Car A enters on a red light and hits Car B, which had a green light. Car A is:",o:["50% at fault","100% at fault","0% at fault","At fault only if charged"],a:1,e:"Under the Fault Determination Rules, the driver who disobeyed the traffic signal is 100% at fault."},
{d:"auto",q:"Who decides the client's percentage of fault for a DCPD claim?",o:["The police officer at the scene","The client's insurer, applying the Fault Determination Rules","The broker","The other driver's insurer only"],a:1,e:"Insurers apply the Fault Determination Rules, not traffic charges, to set fault for DCPD and rating."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the fine range for a first conviction for driving without insurance under the Compulsory Automobile Insurance Act?",o:["$500 to $1,000","$5,000 to $25,000","$50,000 minimum","A warning only"],a:1,e:"A first conviction carries a $5,000 to $25,000 fine, plus possible licence suspension and vehicle impoundment."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's ATV is driven on public trails away from their own property. Does it need liability insurance?",o:["No, off-road vehicles never need insurance","Yes, off-road vehicles generally need insurance when operated off the owner's property","Only if it carries passengers","Only in winter"],a:1,e:"Ontario requires off-road vehicles such as ATVs and snowmobiles to be insured when operated off the owner's land."},
{d:"auto",q:"After an accident, the other driver offers to settle in cash. The client agrees in writing to pay the other driver's damages without telling the insurer. Under the statutory conditions:",o:["That's fine and the insurer must reimburse","The insured must not voluntarily assume liability or settle claims except at their own cost","The insurer must defend anyway","The broker must approve it"],a:1,e:"The statutory conditions bar the insured from admitting liability or settling claims without the insurer, except at their own expense."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client moves from a small town to downtown Toronto and starts commuting 50 km a day. This is:",o:["Not relevant to auto insurance","A material change that should be reported promptly","Only relevant at renewal","Only relevant for home insurance"],a:1,e:"Rating territory and use are material. The statutory conditions require prompt notice of material changes."},
{d:"auto",q:"Under Section 7 on a total loss, what does the insurer normally pay, without OPCF 43?",o:["The original purchase price","The actual cash value at the time of loss","The loan balance","The cost of a new car"],a:1,e:"Standard settlement is actual cash value. OPCF 43 changes this for newer vehicles, for a set period."},
{d:"auto",q:"An OPCF 40 does what?",o:["Adds a deductible to fire and theft losses","Removes theft coverage","Adds accident forgiveness","Excludes a driver"],a:0,e:"OPCF 40 applies a deductible to fire and theft losses, often used to lower the premium on specified-perils style coverage."},
{d:"auto",q:"A driver is stopped by police. What proof of insurance must they be able to show?",o:["The full policy wording","A valid liability insurance card (paper or electronic, where accepted)","The broker's business card","Nothing"],a:1,e:"Drivers must carry proof of insurance, the liability card. Ontario accepts electronic proof where the insurer offers it."},
{d:"auto",q:"Which statement about Ontario's no-fault accident benefits is correct?",o:["They're paid only if the other driver is at fault","They're paid by your own insurer regardless of who caused the accident","They replace lawsuits completely","They only cover property damage"],a:1,e:"Accident benefits are 'no-fault': your own insurer pays them whoever was at fault. Lawsuits for serious injuries are still possible, subject to thresholds."},
// HABITATIONAL
{d:"hab",q:"A client's laptop is stolen from their hotel room on a trip to Spain. Under a typical homeowners policy:",o:["Not covered outside Canada","Covered under Coverage C while temporarily away from the premises, subject to limits and the deductible","Covered under the auto policy","Covered only with travel insurance"],a:1,e:"Personal property is usually covered anywhere in the world while temporarily away from home, sometimes with a lower limit."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's laptop is stolen from their locked car. Which policy normally responds?",o:["The auto policy, Section 7","The homeowners or tenants policy, Coverage C","DCPD","Nothing"],a:1,e:"Auto policies cover the car, not personal belongings in it. Contents are covered under the home or tenant policy."},
{d:"hab",q:"Police order a neighbourhood evacuated for several days because of a nearby gas leak. The client's home isn't damaged. Which coverage may pay hotel costs?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage D, under its civil authority provision, for a limited period","Coverage E","None"],a:1,e:"Many wordings extend additional living expense when a civil authority bars access because of damage nearby, often for up to two weeks."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's dog bites a neighbour's child, who sues. Which coverage responds?",o:["Coverage C","Coverage E, Legal Liability","Coverage H","Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Legal liability covers bodily injury to others caused by the insured's negligence, including animals they own."},
{d:"hab",q:"The insured's own teenage son is hurt tripping on a loose step at home. Does Coverage E pay his injury claim?",o:["Yes, in full","No. Liability coverage excludes injury to the insured and household members","Only if he sues","Only under Coverage F"],a:1,e:"Liability and voluntary medical payments don't cover injuries to people insured under the same policy."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowners policy has a $1,000 deductible. Which coverage does it normally apply to?",o:["Property losses, such as Coverage A, B and C","Legal liability claims","Voluntary medical payments","All coverages equally"],a:0,e:"Deductibles apply to property losses. Liability and voluntary payment coverages usually have no deductible."},
{d:"hab",q:"A house has a market value of $900,000. The land is worth $500,000, and the cost to rebuild is $350,000. The Coverage A limit should be based on:",o:["$900,000","$500,000","$350,000, the reconstruction cost","$400,000"],a:2,e:"Insure the building for its replacement (rebuild) cost. Land isn't insured, and market value includes it."},
{d:"hab",q:"What does a guaranteed replacement cost provision do?",o:["Pays market value","Pays the full cost to rebuild even above the Coverage A limit, if the insured met the insurer's insurance-to-value and notification conditions","Waives the deductible","Covers floods automatically"],a:1,e:"Guaranteed replacement cost depends on the home being insured to the insurer's valuation and changes being reported."},
{d:"hab",q:"After a fire, the city requires the rebuilt house to meet the current building code, which adds $40,000. Without the right endorsement, this extra cost is:",o:["Always covered","Usually excluded unless the policy has by-laws coverage","Covered under Coverage D","Paid by the city"],a:1,e:"By-laws coverage pays the extra cost of complying with building codes and by-laws when rebuilding."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client installs a wood-burning stove in the living room. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing","Treat it as a material change, tell the insurer, and expect an inspection (such as WETT) to be required","Cancel the policy","Add overland water"],a:1,e:"Solid-fuel heating increases fire risk. Insurers usually want it disclosed and professionally inspected."},
{d:"hab",q:"A home has a mortgage, and a covered loss occurs. The claim payment for the building is normally:",o:["Paid only to the insured","Made out to the insured and the mortgagee, as their interests appear","Paid only to the broker","Held by RIBO"],a:1,e:"The mortgage clause protects the lender's interest, and building claim cheques normally include the mortgagee."},
{d:"hab",q:"A condo owner replaced the builder's carpet with premium hardwood and custom cabinets. Whose policy covers these upgrades?",o:["The condo corporation's policy","The unit owner's policy, under improvements and betterments","No policy covers upgrades","The builder's warranty"],a:1,e:"The corporation insures the standard unit. Owner upgrades are covered under the unit owner's improvements and betterments coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"What does a condominium corporation's master policy usually cover?",o:["Owners' personal belongings","Common elements and the standard units as built","Owners' personal liability","Owners' additional living expenses"],a:1,e:"The master policy covers the building, common elements and standard unit finishes. Owners insure contents, upgrades, their own liability and assessments."},
{d:"hab",q:"An earthquake damages a client's home in Ontario. Under a standard comprehensive homeowners policy:",o:["Covered automatically","Excluded unless an earthquake endorsement was added","Covered only for contents","Covered under Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Earthquake is a standard exclusion that can be bought back by endorsement."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client spills paint on an expensive rug. On which form is this most likely covered?",o:["Basic form (named perils on contents)","Comprehensive form (all risks on contents)","Neither form","Only on a tenant's form"],a:1,e:"Accidental damage like a spill isn't a named peril. All-risks coverage on contents responds unless it's specifically excluded."},
{d:"hab",q:"A slow drip under a bathroom sink went unnoticed for months and rotted the subfloor. Under most wordings, the rot damage is:",o:["Covered as sudden escape of water","Excluded as continuous or repeated seepage, wear and tear, and rot","Covered under sewer backup","Covered under Coverage E"],a:1,e:"Policies cover sudden and accidental water escape, not gradual leakage, rot or mould from long-term seepage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client plans to gut and renovate their house, and nobody will live there for four months. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing, because renovations are normal","Tell the insurer. Major renovations and vacancy change the risk and may need special terms","Cancel the policy","Add OPCF 16"],a:1,e:"Renovations increase fire and theft risk, and vacancy limits can apply. Insurers must be told so they can adjust the coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"Theft from a seasonal cottage is often covered only if:",o:["The cottage is worth more than $500,000","There are visible signs of forced entry, depending on the wording","It happens in summer","A police report is filed within 1 year"],a:1,e:"Seasonal and secondary policies often restrict theft, for example to forcible entry. Read the specific wording."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's sump pump fails during a storm and water floods the basement. Which coverage usually responds?",o:["Overland water only","A sewer backup or water damage endorsement that includes sump pump failure","Coverage E","Earthquake"],a:1,e:"Sump pump failure and overflow are usually handled under the sewer backup or water endorsement. Check the endorsement's definitions."},
// COMMERCIAL
{d:"com",q:"Two partners run a landscaping business as a general partnership. A large judgment exceeds the business's assets. Who is at risk?",o:["Neither partner personally","Both partners, personally","Only the partner who caused the loss","Only the business's insurer"],a:1,e:"General partners are usually personally liable for partnership debts, including judgments."},
{d:"com",q:"A corporation is sued and loses more than its assets. A shareholder's personal liability is generally:",o:["Unlimited","Limited to what they invested in the corporation","Equal to the judgment","Shared with the directors"],a:1,e:"A corporation is a separate legal entity, so shareholders' liability is generally limited to their investment."},
{d:"com",q:"A clothing store's stock is destroyed by fire. Under a standard commercial property form, stock is usually valued at:",o:["Its retail selling price","The insured's cost to replace it","Wholesale market average","The bank's appraisal"],a:1,e:"Stock is usually valued at the insured's replacement cost, not selling price. Lost profit is a business interruption issue."},
{d:"com",q:"Road construction blocks access to a café for three weeks. The café isn't damaged. Does standard business interruption pay?",o:["Yes, always","No. BI usually needs direct physical damage from an insured peril, unless an extension such as ingress/egress applies","Yes, through CGL","Only if police close the road"],a:1,e:"BI is triggered by physical loss or damage from an insured peril. Access-related extensions are limited and need specific wording."},
{d:"com",q:"A manufacturer's only supplier of a key part suffers a fire, and the manufacturer has to stop production. Which coverage addresses this?",o:["Equipment breakdown","Contingent business interruption","Crime","Builders' risk"],a:1,e:"Contingent (dependent property) BI covers income loss from damage at a key supplier's or customer's premises."},
{d:"com",q:"A company's delivery truck causes an accident. Why won't the company's CGL respond?",o:["CGL excludes liability arising from owned automobiles, which need an auto policy","CGL only covers bodily injury","CGL only covers premises","The driver was at fault"],a:0,e:"CGL excludes owned automobiles. Auto liability is insured under the OAP 1 or a fleet policy."},
{d:"com",q:"A store's security guard wrongly detains a customer for shoplifting. The customer sues for false arrest. Which CGL coverage applies?",o:["Coverage A, Bodily Injury and Property Damage","Coverage B, Personal and Advertising Injury","Coverage C, Medical Payments","Coverage D, Tenants' Legal Liability"],a:1,e:"Coverage B covers personal injury offences such as false arrest, libel, slander and invasion of privacy."},
{d:"com",q:"A customer trips in a store and needs minor medical treatment. The store wants to pay without admitting fault. Which CGL coverage fits?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage B","Coverage C, Medical Payments","Coverage D"],a:2,e:"Medical payments pays reasonable medical expenses regardless of fault, which helps avoid lawsuits."},
{d:"com",q:"A contractor builds a deck. Six months after the job is finished, the deck collapses and injures guests. Which part of the contractor's CGL responds?",o:["Premises hazard","Products-completed operations hazard","Tenants' legal liability","Crime"],a:1,e:"Products-completed operations covers injury or damage from completed work or sold products after the insured has left the job."},
{d:"com",q:"A landlord requires a tenant's CGL to protect the landlord directly. How is this done properly?",o:["Issue a certificate only","Add the landlord as an additional insured by endorsement","Name the landlord as the policy owner","Buy a second CGL"],a:1,e:"Only an endorsement extends coverage to the landlord. A certificate just shows the coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A business has $2 million CGL and $1 million auto liability, and wants $5 million more protection over both. Recommend:",o:["Umbrella liability policy","Crime policy","Builders' risk","D&O"],a:0,e:"An umbrella sits above the primary liability policies, adds higher limits and may broaden coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"After a data breach, a business pays for IT forensics, customer notifications and credit monitoring. Which type of cyber coverage pays these costs?",o:["Third-party cyber liability","First-party cyber coverage","CGL Coverage B","Crime"],a:1,e:"First-party cyber pays the business's own costs (forensics, notification, restoration, ransom, BI). Third-party pays claims by others."},
{d:"com",q:"A restaurant's walk-in freezer compressor burns out, and $8,000 of food spoils. Which coverage is designed for this?",o:["Equipment breakdown with spoilage coverage","CGL","Builders' risk","Fidelity"],a:0,e:"Equipment breakdown covers sudden mechanical or electrical failure, and often the resulting spoilage and business interruption."},
{d:"com",q:"When does builders' risk coverage usually end?",o:["After 30 days","When the building is completed, occupied or put to its intended use","Never","When the contractor is paid"],a:1,e:"Builders' risk is temporary and ends at completion or occupancy. Permanent property coverage must then take over."},
{d:"com",q:"A plumber's tools and equipment move between job sites every day. Which coverage fits best?",o:["Building coverage","Contractor's equipment floater","Builders' risk","Tenants' legal liability"],a:1,e:"Floaters cover mobile property wherever it is. Building forms are limited to the described premises."},
{d:"com",q:"A trucking company wants protection for its liability for customers' goods it hauls. Recommend:",o:["Motor truck cargo","Commercial property","D&O","Trade credit"],a:0,e:"Motor truck cargo covers a carrier's legal liability for goods in its care while in transit."},
{d:"com",q:"A manufacturer ships its own products by sea to Europe. Which coverage protects the goods?",o:["Motor truck cargo","Marine cargo","Builders' risk","Garage policy"],a:1,e:"Marine cargo insures the owner's goods in transit by sea (and often connected land transit)."},
{d:"com",q:"A wholesaler worries that a major customer will go bankrupt before paying $300,000 of invoices. Which coverage addresses this?",o:["Trade credit insurance","Crime","Business interruption","Surety"],a:0,e:"Trade credit insurance protects accounts receivable against customer insolvency or non-payment."},
{d:"com",q:"A city requires a contractor to guarantee that it will finish a building project according to the contract. Which bond is this?",o:["Bid bond","Performance bond","Labour and material payment bond","Fidelity bond"],a:1,e:"A performance bond guarantees completion according to contract terms. A bid bond guarantees the bidder will sign. A payment bond guarantees subcontractors and suppliers get paid."},
{d:"com",q:"A business rents vans for a week-long job, and the rental company holds the business responsible for damage. Which endorsement on the commercial auto policy addresses this?",o:["OPCF 27B","OPCF 28A","OPCF 44R","OPCF 49"],a:0,e:"OPCF 27B is the business version of legal liability for damage to non-owned automobiles, such as rented vehicles used in the business."},
{d:"com",q:"Which document shows a driver's licence status, convictions and suspensions, and is often used to check commercial driver eligibility?",o:["Driver's abstract","Certificate of insurance","CVOR certificate","Bill of lading"],a:0,e:"A driver's abstract from the Ministry of Transportation shows the driving record. Insurers use it to decide driver eligibility."},
{d:"com",q:"A consultant is sued for giving faulty advice that cost a client money. No one was hurt and no property was damaged. Which coverage responds?",o:["CGL Coverage A","Professional liability (E&O)","Crime","Equipment breakdown"],a:1,e:"Financial loss from professional advice is an E&O exposure. CGL excludes professional services and pure financial loss."},
{d:"com",q:"An architect with a claims-made policy retires and cancels it. A year later a former client sues over earlier work. What would have protected the architect?",o:["Nothing","An extended reporting period ('tail') on the claims-made policy","An occurrence CGL","A surety bond"],a:1,e:"An extended reporting period lets claims made after cancellation be reported, for work done while the policy was in force."},
{d:"com",q:"A claims-made policy has a retroactive date of January 1, 2023. A claim made in 2026 arises from work done in 2021. The claim is:",o:["Covered","Not covered, because the work was before the retroactive date","Covered at half the limit","Covered only by the CGL"],a:1,e:"Claims-made policies exclude claims from acts before the retroactive date. Keep coverage continuous so the retro date doesn't move forward."},
{d:"com",q:"A commercial building sits vacant for 45 days while the owner looks for a new tenant. What is the main insurance concern?",o:["None","Vacancy provisions may restrict or suspend some coverage after a set period, often 30 days","The CGL lapses","The co-insurance clause doubles"],a:1,e:"Commercial property forms typically restrict perils like vandalism, glass breakage and water damage after a vacancy period. Tell the insurer."},
{d:"com",q:"A restaurant tenant spends $80,000 on a new kitchen fit-out in a rented space. Which coverage on the tenant's policy protects this investment?",o:["Tenant's improvements and betterments","The landlord's building policy","Builders' risk","Crime"],a:0,e:"Tenants insure the improvements they paid for under improvements and betterments. The landlord's policy normally covers only the base building."},
{d:"com",q:"What is the main purpose of a commercial insurance broker using a building valuation tool or appraisal?",o:["To estimate market value for a sale","To set a building limit at reconstruction cost, avoiding underinsurance and co-insurance penalties","To lower the deductible","To get a mortgage"],a:1,e:"Building limits should reflect rebuild cost. Underinsuring triggers co-insurance penalties and uninsured losses."},
{d:"com",q:"A cleaning company's employee steals jewellery from a client's home while cleaning. Which coverage protects the cleaning company's client?",o:["CGL Coverage A","Third-party crime (employee dishonesty for clients' property), often bought by service businesses","Builders' risk","Business interruption"],a:1,e:"Employee dishonesty can be extended to clients' property. Service businesses often need it to win contracts."},
{d:"com",q:"A volunteer board member of a non-profit is sued for a decision the board made. Which coverage protects them?",o:["CGL","D&O (directors and officers) liability","Equipment breakdown","Commercial auto"],a:1,e:"D&O protects directors and officers, including volunteer board members, for wrongful acts in governing the organization."},
{d:"com",q:"Why might an insurer insist that a commercial client's fleet drivers meet minimum licence and record standards?",o:["RIBO requires it","Driver eligibility is a key underwriting factor in commercial auto risk","To set the CGL premium","It is required for DCPD"],a:1,e:"Commercial auto underwriting depends heavily on who drives. Insurers set eligibility rules for experience, licence class and convictions."},
// TRAVEL
{d:"trv",q:"A client's parents are visiting from abroad for three months and have no provincial health coverage. Which product fits?",o:["Single-trip travel medical for Canadian residents","Visitors to Canada emergency medical insurance","Trip cancellation only","Their child's group plan"],a:1,e:"Visitors to Canada plans cover emergency medical costs for people who don't have provincial health coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client takes 8 short business trips to the U.S. each year. What's the most efficient option, and what should the broker check?",o:["Separate single-trip policies, no checks needed","An annual multi-trip plan, checking the maximum days allowed per trip","No coverage, because the trips are short","Trip cancellation only"],a:1,e:"Annual multi-trip plans cover unlimited trips, but each trip is limited to a set number of days."},
{d:"trv",q:"While on vacation, a client's spouse is hospitalized at home in Canada, and the client must fly back early. Which coverage pays the extra travel costs?",o:["Trip cancellation","Trip interruption","Baggage","Emergency medical"],a:1,e:"Trip interruption covers extra costs to return early, or unused prepaid costs, after departure for a covered reason."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client's group benefits plan covers trips up to 15 days, and they're going away for 30 days. What should the broker suggest?",o:["Nothing, because the group plan covers everything","A top-up policy for the days beyond the group plan's limit","Cancel the group plan","Trip cancellation only"],a:1,e:"Top-up plans extend coverage beyond the days covered by a group or credit card plan."},
{d:"trv",q:"A snowbird plans to spend 8 months outside Canada this year. What is the main risk?",o:["None","Being away longer than OHIP allows (212 days in a 12-month period) could make them ineligible for OHIP, which most travel medical policies require","Only baggage limits","Their auto policy lapses"],a:1,e:"OHIP allows up to 212 days outside Canada in a 12-month period. Most travel medical policies need valid provincial health coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client is injured while skydiving on vacation. Many travel medical policies:",o:["Always cover it","Exclude or limit injuries from high-risk activities unless coverage was added","Pay double","Only cover it in Canada"],a:1,e:"High-risk activities such as skydiving and some extreme sports are commonly excluded or need a rider."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client relies on their credit card's travel medical coverage. What should the broker check?",o:["Nothing, because cards always cover everything","Age limits, maximum trip length and coverage limits, which are often restrictive","Only the card's interest rate","Whether the card is a Visa"],a:1,e:"Credit card coverage often has age caps, short trip limits and pre-existing condition rules. Compare it with the client's actual trip."},
// ===== SET 3 =====
// SET 3 — GENERAL
{d:"gen",q:"A client has two home policies by mistake, each with a $400,000 dwelling limit. A $100,000 fire occurs. How much can the client collect in total?",o:["$200,000","$100,000, shared between the insurers","$400,000","$800,000"],a:1,e:"Indemnity limits recovery to the actual loss. Contribution splits it between the two insurers."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client asks why insurance exists at all. The BEST answer is:",o:["To make insurers rich","To spread the cost of losses among many people so no one is ruined by one event","To replace savings","To fund the government"],a:1,e:"Insurance pools risk. Many small premiums pay for the few large losses."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which situation shows a lack of insurable interest?",o:["A homeowner insuring their house","A bank insuring a car it financed","A person insuring a stranger's house they have no financial connection to","A business insuring its stock"],a:2,e:"Without a financial stake in the property, there's no insurable interest, and the policy would amount to gambling."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which of these is a 'peril'?",o:["Old wiring","Lightning","A careless owner","A house near a river"],a:1,e:"A peril is a cause of loss, such as lightning. The other options are hazards."},
{d:"gen",q:"A homeowner leaves the doors unlocked and keys in the car. This is BEST described as:",o:["A moral hazard","A physical hazard","A peril","Subrogation"],a:0,e:"Carelessness that increases loss risk, sometimes called morale hazard, is grouped with moral hazard on most exams."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which term means the insurer's right to keep and sell damaged property after paying a total loss?",o:["Salvage","Subrogation","Contribution","Utmost good faith"],a:0,e:"Salvage lets the insurer recover value from damaged property it has paid for, such as a written-off car."},
{d:"gen",q:"After paying a claim, the insurer recovers money from the at-fault party. The insured's deductible is also recovered. What usually happens with it?",o:["The insurer keeps it","The deductible is usually reimbursed to the insured from the recovery, depending on the policy","RIBO keeps it","The broker keeps it"],a:1,e:"Insurers commonly repay the insured's deductible from subrogation recoveries, depending on the wording and the amount recovered."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which is an example of the law of large numbers in insurance?",o:["One person's claims history","The more similar risks an insurer pools, the more accurately it can predict losses","Insuring only one large building","Setting rates by guessing"],a:1,e:"Large pools make losses more predictable, which lets insurers price fairly."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer has a combined ratio over 100%. What does that mean?",o:["It made an underwriting profit","Its claims plus expenses exceeded its premiums, which is an underwriting loss","Its loss ratio is 0%","It is bankrupt"],a:1,e:"The combined ratio is the loss ratio plus the expense ratio. Over 100% means underwriting lost money, though investment income can offset it."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which party in an insurance transaction works for the client?",o:["The underwriter","The broker","The staff adjuster","The reinsurer"],a:1,e:"Brokers represent clients. Underwriters and staff adjusters work for the insurer."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer that's licensed in another country but not in Ontario offers cheap coverage to a client. What should the broker consider?",o:["Place it right away","Placing with unlicensed insurers has strict rules and risks, such as weak claims protection, and the broker must follow the law and disclose risks","It's always better","RIBO requires it"],a:1,e:"Unlicensed insurers aren't regulated here. Placing business with them involves legal requirements and client protection risks."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's the main role of the property and casualty insurer compensation fund (for example, PACICC) in Canada?",o:["Regulating brokers","Protecting policyholders if a property and casualty insurer becomes insolvent, within limits","Setting auto rates","Running the Facility Association"],a:1,e:"The industry compensation plan (PACICC, the Property and Casualty Insurance Compensation Corporation) protects policyholders when a member insurer fails."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which statement about Ontario brokers and FSRA is correct?",o:["FSRA licenses brokers","RIBO licenses brokers. FSRA regulates insurers and agents and some market conduct, such as auto rates","Neither regulates insurance","Brokers are regulated federally"],a:1,e:"Broker licensing is RIBO's role. FSRA regulates insurers, agents and auto rates."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker wants to offer a client a gift card for every new client they refer. What should the broker do FIRST?",o:["Start immediately","Check RIBO's rules on referral fees and incentives, and any brokerage policy","Ask the client to keep it secret","Pay it in cash"],a:1,e:"Referral fees and inducements are regulated. Brokers must follow RIBO's rules and disclose where required."},
{d:"gen",q:"A Level 1 broker makes a mistake that causes a client's loss. Who is also accountable for supervising the broker?",o:["Nobody","The Principal Broker and the brokerage","FSRA","The insurer"],a:1,e:"Level 1 brokers act under the supervision of a Principal Broker, who is responsible for oversight."},
{d:"gen",q:"Under RIBO's rules, which of these is an example of misconduct?",o:["Explaining a policy clearly","Misrepresenting a policy's coverage to make a sale","Disclosing a conflict of interest","Keeping accurate records"],a:1,e:"Misrepresenting coverage is professional misconduct under the code of conduct."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker finds out a coworker is using client premiums to pay personal bills. What should the broker do?",o:["Ignore it","Report it to the brokerage's Principal Broker or management, and to RIBO if required","Join in","Tell the client to pay again"],a:1,e:"Misuse of trust funds is serious misconduct. Brokers must report non-compliance."},
{d:"gen",q:"How long must brokers generally keep client records?",o:["30 days","For the period set out in RIBO's record-keeping requirements","Never","Only until the policy ends"],a:1,e:"RIBO sets minimum record retention periods. Records protect clients and support E&O defence."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker's personal social media post promises 'the lowest price guaranteed on every policy'. What's the concern?",o:["None","Advertising must not be misleading, and promises like this may breach RIBO's advertising rules","Brokers can't use social media","Only insurers can advertise"],a:1,e:"Broker advertising must be truthful and not misleading under RIBO's rules."},
{d:"gen",q:"Under the Fair Treatment of Customers principles, at which stage of the relationship does fair treatment apply?",o:["Only at the sale","At every stage: design, promotion, advice, sale, servicing and claims","Only at claims","Only at renewal"],a:1,e:"Fair treatment applies throughout the life cycle of the product and the relationship."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client who speaks limited English buys a policy. What's the broker's BEST practice?",o:["Rush through the sale","Take extra care that the client understands, using plain language or an interpreter and confirming understanding","Avoid explaining exclusions","Tell them to sign quickly"],a:1,e:"Fair treatment means making sure clients understand what they buy, especially vulnerable clients."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client refuses a coverage the broker recommends. What should the broker do?",o:["Argue until they agree","Respect the decision, explain the consequences, and document it, ideally with the client's written acknowledgement","Add it anyway","Cancel the client"],a:1,e:"Clients decide. Documenting declined coverage protects both client and broker."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker is asked by a friend to backdate a policy so a loss that already happened is covered. What should the broker do?",o:["Do it as a favour","Refuse. It's fraud and serious misconduct","Do it for a fee","Ask the insurer to look away"],a:1,e:"Backdating coverage after a known loss is fraud."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's the purpose of a binder in insurance?",o:["A temporary contract giving coverage until the policy is issued","A claims form","A complaint letter","A RIBO licence"],a:0,e:"A binder confirms temporary coverage, within binding authority, until the formal policy is issued."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurer issues an endorsement to a policy. What is an endorsement?",o:["A complaint","A written change to the policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage","A RIBO notice","A premium invoice"],a:1,e:"Endorsements change the base policy. They're common in auto (OPCFs) and property policies."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's the difference between a 'condition' and an 'exclusion' in a policy?",o:["They're the same","Exclusions remove coverage for certain events. Conditions are rules both parties must follow, like reporting a claim","Conditions are only in auto","Exclusions are optional"],a:1,e:"Exclusions narrow coverage. Conditions set duties and procedures."},
{d:"gen",q:"What does 'pro-rata' cancellation mean?",o:["The insured gets back the exact unused share of premium","The insurer keeps a penalty","No refund","Double refund"],a:0,e:"Pro-rata returns the unused premium exactly. Short-rate keeps a penalty."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which describes a 'direct writer'?",o:["An insurer that sells directly to the public through its own employees","A broker","An MGA","A reinsurer"],a:0,e:"Direct writers sell their own products without independent intermediaries."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which describes a 'captive agent'?",o:["An intermediary who represents only one insurer","A broker who represents clients","A claims adjuster","A regulator"],a:0,e:"Captive agents sell one company's products. Brokers access many markets."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker places business with an MGA. What should the broker understand?",o:["MGAs are regulators","The MGA has authority from insurers to underwrite or bind, and the broker should know which insurer actually carries the risk","MGAs don't need insurers","MGAs are always unlicensed"],a:1,e:"MGAs act on insurers' behalf. Brokers should disclose the actual insurer and check its security."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's an 'insurance-to-value' problem?",o:["Insuring something for too little compared with its real value","Paying premiums late","A RIBO complaint","A fraud case"],a:0,e:"Underinsurance means claims may not be fully paid, and co-insurance penalties may apply."},
{d:"gen",q:"Why do insurers price differently in different regions?",o:["They don't","Loss experience varies by region, such as theft rates, weather and repair costs","RIBO requires it","Only for commercial lines"],a:1,e:"Geography affects risk. Pricing reflects regional loss experience."},
{d:"gen",q:"What is 'secondary perils' in industry discussions?",o:["Perils after the first claim","More frequent, smaller-scale catastrophe events like hail, floods and wildfires that drive rising losses","Perils excluded by RIBO","Perils covered only by D&O"],a:1,e:"Secondary perils are frequent, smaller catastrophes. Their growing cost affects pricing and availability."},
{d:"gen",q:"How does new car technology affect auto insurance costs?",o:["It always lowers costs","Advanced sensors and parts can make repairs much more expensive, even though they may prevent some accidents","No effect","It removes the need for insurance"],a:1,e:"Technology both reduces some accidents and raises repair costs, affecting premiums."},
{d:"gen",q:"Why does insurance fraud matter to honest clients?",o:["It doesn't","Fraud raises claims costs, which raises everyone's premiums","It lowers premiums","Only insurers pay"],a:1,e:"Fraud costs are passed on to all policyholders through higher premiums."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker suspects a client's application has a false address to get a lower rate. What should the broker do?",o:["Process it","Not submit false information, discuss it with the client, and follow brokerage procedures, including reporting if needed","Ignore it","Change it themselves"],a:1,e:"Brokers can't knowingly submit false information. Rate evasion is a form of fraud."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which describes 'proximate cause' in a chain of events?",o:["The last event before the loss","The dominant, effective cause that started the chain leading to the loss","Any cause listed in the policy","The cause the insured prefers"],a:1,e:"Proximate cause looks at the dominant cause, not simply the last event in time."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's 'consideration' in an insurance contract?",o:["The premium paid by the insured and the insurer's promise to pay covered losses","The broker's commission","The deductible","A courtesy"],a:0,e:"Consideration is what each party gives. For insurance, the premium is exchanged for the insurer's promise."},
{d:"gen",q:"A 15-year-old signs an insurance contract alone. What's the concern?",o:["None","Legal capacity: minors may not have full capacity to contract","They must pay double","RIBO forbids it"],a:1,e:"A valid contract requires parties with legal capacity. Minors' contracts can be voidable."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurance policy is described as a 'unilateral contract'. What does that mean?",o:["Only the insurer makes an enforceable promise to pay, while the insured isn't forced to keep paying","Both sides must perform equally","It's only for businesses","It can't be cancelled"],a:0,e:"The insurer promises to pay covered losses. The insured can stop paying, though coverage then ends."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurance policy is 'aleatory'. What does that mean?",o:["Payment depends on an uncertain event, so amounts exchanged may be unequal","It has no exclusions","It's always the same price","It covers everything"],a:0,e:"Aleatory contracts depend on chance. One client may pay premiums for years without a claim, another may claim right away."},
{d:"gen",q:"What's the main difference between a Level 1 and higher RIBO licences?",o:["None","Level 1 acts under supervision. Higher levels allow more independence, and Level 3 allows managing a brokerage, with more experience and exams","Level 1 can manage a brokerage","Level 1 is only for life insurance"],a:1,e:"RIBO's licence levels reflect experience and responsibility. Level 1 is entry level and supervised."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker finishes a course on ethics. Why might RIBO care?",o:["It doesn't","RIBO requires continuing education, and some credits may need to be in specific areas","It replaces the exam","It's needed for auto rates"],a:1,e:"RIBO's continuing education requirements keep brokers current. Check the current rules on hours and categories."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which is a good example of 'continuous learning' for a new broker?",o:["Never reading industry news","Reading insurer bulletins and industry publications, and asking experienced brokers questions","Only studying for the licence exam","Avoiding CE"],a:1,e:"The competency profile expects brokers to keep up with industry changes and learn from others."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client asks the broker to keep their claim history off an application because 'it was a long time ago'. What should the broker do?",o:["Leave it off","Explain that it must be disclosed if asked or material, and that hiding it risks the coverage","Lie for them","Change the dates"],a:1,e:"Material facts must be disclosed. Hiding them can void the policy and expose the broker."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which law requires businesses to identify the purposes for which personal information is collected?",o:["CASL","PIPEDA","The Highway Traffic Act","The Insurance Act only"],a:1,e:"PIPEDA (the Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act) requires identifying purposes, consent, limits on use and safeguards."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client asks to see the personal information the brokerage holds about them. Under PIPEDA:",o:["The brokerage can refuse for any reason","Individuals generally have a right to access their personal information, subject to limited exceptions","Only RIBO can see it","Only the insurer can respond"],a:1,e:"PIPEDA gives individuals a right to access their personal information and ask for corrections."},
{d:"gen",q:"A brokerage stores client files on a laptop with no password. What's the concern?",o:["None","Failing to safeguard personal information breaches privacy duties and raises cyber risk","It's faster","It's required by RIBO"],a:1,e:"Brokers must protect client data with appropriate safeguards like passwords, encryption and access controls."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker texts a promotion to a list of people who never agreed to receive messages. Which law is MOST likely breached?",o:["PIPEDA only","CASL, Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation","The Insurance Act","The Highway Traffic Act"],a:1,e:"CASL requires consent and identification for commercial electronic messages."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client pays by credit card over the phone. Which standard governs how the card details must be handled?",o:["PCI DSS (the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard)","CASL","The SABS","The OAP 1"],a:0,e:"PCI DSS sets security rules for handling payment card data."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which describes 'duty of care' for a broker?",o:["No duty","A legal obligation to act with the skill and care of a reasonable broker when advising and placing coverage","A duty to the insurer only","Only a moral duty"],a:1,e:"Brokers owe clients a duty of care. Falling below the standard can be negligence and lead to E&O claims."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client's claim is denied because the broker placed the wrong coverage. What may the client do?",o:["Nothing","Sue the broker for negligence, which the broker's E&O insurance may defend","Only complain to IBAO","Sue RIBO"],a:1,e:"Clients can hold brokers liable for errors. That's why E&O insurance and documentation matter."},
{d:"gen",q:"An insurance company is regulated federally for solvency and provincially for market conduct. Which federal body supervises federally regulated insurers' solvency?",o:["OSFI, the Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions","RIBO","IBAO","CCIR"],a:0,e:"OSFI supervises the solvency of federally regulated insurers. Provinces like Ontario, through FSRA, regulate market conduct."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which organization publishes statistics and advocacy for Canada's property and casualty insurers, and drafts many standard home and commercial wordings?",o:["RIBO","The Insurance Bureau of Canada (IBC)","FSRA","CISRO"],a:1,e:"The Insurance Bureau of Canada represents property and casualty insurers and publishes model wordings and data."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker must choose between two insurers with similar quotes. One pays the brokerage a higher commission. What's required?",o:["Choose the higher commission secretly","Recommend based on the client's interests, and disclose compensation arrangements as required","Hide both quotes","Let the insurer decide"],a:1,e:"Brokers must put clients first and disclose compensation conflicts as RIBO requires."},
{d:"gen",q:"A client asks why the broker recommended a higher liability limit than the legal minimum. The BEST explanation is:",o:["To earn more commission","Serious injury lawsuits can easily exceed minimum limits, and the client would pay the difference personally","RIBO requires it","It's the only option"],a:1,e:"Brokers recommend limits that match exposures and explain the reasoning."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which is an example of an open-ended question during a needs analysis?",o:["Do you have a pool?","Tell me how you use your home and property.","Is your roof new?","Do you want sewer backup?"],a:1,e:"Open-ended questions invite detail and uncover exposures. Closed questions confirm specifics."},
{d:"gen",q:"A broker realizes after binding that they quoted the wrong deductible. What should they do?",o:["Hope no one notices","Correct it promptly, tell the client and insurer, and document it","Change it at renewal","Blame the insurer"],a:1,e:"Errors should be fixed and disclosed quickly. This limits harm and E&O exposure."},
{d:"gen",q:"An adjuster asks the broker for the client's file notes to help with a claim. What's the broker's guideline?",o:["Share everything without thinking","Share relevant information as allowed by the client's consent and privacy rules, and help the claim move forward","Refuse everything","Delete the notes"],a:1,e:"Brokers help claims while respecting privacy and consent requirements."},
{d:"gen",q:"Which describes 'utmost good faith' for the INSURER?",o:["It doesn't apply to insurers","The insurer must deal fairly and honestly, including handling claims in good faith","Only the insured must be honest","Only brokers"],a:1,e:"Good faith is mutual. Insurers that handle claims in bad faith can face significant damages."},
// SET 3 — AUTO
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is 0% at fault for a collision in Ontario with another insured car. Their car has $12,000 of damage and their DCPD has no deductible. DCPD pays:",o:["$12,000","$6,000","$0","$9,000"],a:0,e:"Not at fault means DCPD pays 100% of the damage: $12,000."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is 50% at fault. The car damage is $10,000, DCPD has no deductible, and the client has collision with a $1,000 deductible. What is the total paid?",o:["$10,000","$9,000","$5,000","$4,000"],a:1,e:"DCPD pays 50% = $5,000. Collision pays the at-fault 50% ($5,000) minus the $1,000 deductible = $4,000. Total $9,000."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is 100% at fault, has no collision or all perils coverage, and the car damage is $7,000. How much does their policy pay for the car?",o:["$7,000","$3,500","$0","$6,000"],a:2,e:"DCPD pays only the not-at-fault share, which is 0%. Without collision or all perils, there's no coverage for the at-fault damage."},
{d:"auto",q:"DCPD applies only when which conditions are met?",o:["The accident happens anywhere in North America","The collision happens in Ontario with at least one other insured vehicle, and the claim is for the insured's own vehicle damage and loss of use","The other driver is uninsured","Police lay charges"],a:1,e:"DCPD needs an Ontario collision involving another insured automobile. Accidents outside Ontario go under collision or a claim against the other party."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client's car is damaged in a collision in New York State with another car. Which coverage is MOST likely to pay for your client's car?",o:["DCPD","Collision or all perils, or a claim against the at-fault driver","Section 5","Accident benefits"],a:1,e:"DCPD doesn't apply outside Ontario. The client uses collision coverage or claims against the at-fault driver."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client hits a deer on a highway. In Ontario's OAP 1, which coverage normally responds?",o:["DCPD","Comprehensive (or all perils)","Third-party liability","Uninsured automobile"],a:1,e:"Contact with an animal is treated as a comprehensive loss under the OAP 1. Check the current wording."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car is stolen and not recovered. Which coverage pays?",o:["Collision","Comprehensive, specified perils or all perils","DCPD","Section 3"],a:1,e:"Theft is covered under comprehensive, specified perils or all perils."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car rolls over in a ditch on an icy road. No other vehicle is involved. Which coverage pays?",o:["Comprehensive","Collision or upset (or all perils)","DCPD","Uninsured automobile"],a:1,e:"Upset (rollover) and collision with an object are covered under collision or all perils."},
{d:"auto",q:"A tree branch falls on a client's parked car during a windstorm. Which coverage pays?",o:["Collision","Comprehensive (or all perils, or specified perils since windstorm is listed)","DCPD","Third-party liability"],a:1,e:"Falling objects and windstorm are comprehensive perils. Windstorm is also a specified peril."},
{d:"auto",q:"A vandal scratches a client's car in a parking lot. The client has only specified perils coverage. Is it covered?",o:["Yes","No, vandalism isn't a specified peril","Only under DCPD","Only under accident benefits"],a:1,e:"Vandalism is covered under comprehensive or all perils, not specified perils."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the purpose of the deductible under Section 7, loss or damage?",o:["It applies to injuries","The insured pays the first part of each physical damage claim","It applies to liability claims","It's paid by the other driver"],a:1,e:"Section 7 deductibles apply to physical damage to the insured car, such as collision and comprehensive."},
{d:"auto",q:"Does third-party liability (Section 3) pay for damage to the insured's own car?",o:["Yes","No, it pays for others' injuries and property damage that the insured is legally liable for","Only if the insured is 50% at fault","Only on weekends"],a:1,e:"Liability coverage protects others. The insured's own car damage is covered by DCPD or Section 7."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client lends their car to a friend with a valid licence who causes an accident. Generally, whose policy responds first for liability?",o:["The friend's policy only","The car owner's policy, because insurance generally follows the car","RIBO","No one"],a:1,e:"In Ontario, the owner's policy is generally primary for its vehicle. The driver's own policy may be excess."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's spouse is a licensed driver but rarely drives. The client asks if they need to be listed. What's correct?",o:["No, only frequent drivers","Yes, licensed household members and anyone with regular access should be disclosed","Only if they have accidents","Only for commercial vehicles"],a:1,e:"Insurers need all licensed drivers in the household and regular drivers. Not disclosing them can be a misrepresentation."},
{d:"auto",q:"The insurer sends a renewal notice with a 20% premium increase. Is the insurer required to renew under the Take All Comers rule?",o:["Yes, at the same price","Insurers can change rates using approved rates, and non-renew only for reasons allowed by their approved rules","No rules apply","Only if the client complains"],a:1,e:"Insurers set premiums using FSRA-approved rates and can only decline or non-renew within their approved underwriting rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car has a dashcam and a telematics device that tracks driving habits. Telematics programs are mainly used to:",o:["Replace the OAP 1","Offer discounts based on actual driving behaviour","Determine fault for DCPD","Track stolen cars only"],a:1,e:"Usage-based insurance (telematics) rates drivers on things like braking, speed and time of day, often with a discount."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the purpose of an OPCF 19 on a private passenger car?",o:["To limit the amount payable for the insured vehicle to a stated value","To add accident benefits","To exclude a driver","To add rental coverage"],a:0,e:"OPCF 19 limits the amount payable for loss or damage to a stated amount, often used for older or unusual vehicles."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client owns a rare classic car worth far more than standard valuation tools show. Which approach protects its value?",o:["An agreed value endorsement, such as OPCF 19A, with an appraisal","OPCF 28A","DCPD","OPCF 16"],a:0,e:"Agreed value endorsements set the payout in advance, based on an appraisal, so the client avoids disputes about actual cash value."},
{d:"auto",q:"A family's teenager is excluded with an OPCF 28A. The teenager drives the car anyway and injures a pedestrian. What is the result for the owner's own coverage?",o:["Full coverage","The excluded driver's use isn't covered for the owner's own coverages, and the insurer may pay third parties and recover from the owner","Only accident benefits for the owner","DCPD pays everything"],a:1,e:"An excluded driver is excluded from coverage. Minimum third-party protection may still be paid to victims, and the insurer can seek recovery. Owners must understand this risk."},
{d:"auto",q:"OPCF 28 (reduction of coverage for named persons) differs from OPCF 28A because OPCF 28:",o:["Excludes all coverage","Reduces coverage for a named person to specified levels instead of excluding them entirely","Adds a driver","Adds rental coverage"],a:1,e:"OPCF 28 reduces coverage when a named person drives. OPCF 28A is a full exclusion."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client leases a car, and the leasing company requires protection of its interest and liability limits of at least $2 million. What should the broker provide?",o:["An OPCF 5 only","Lessor protection (such as OPCF 23A and the lessor's requirements, plus OPCF 5 if the insured is the lessor) and the required liability limit","SPF 6","Nothing"],a:1,e:"Leasing companies require their interest to be protected and minimum limits. Brokers should check the lease and endorse the policy as required."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car is written off. They still owe $25,000 on the loan, but the actual cash value is $20,000. Without gap coverage or OPCF 43, who bears the $5,000 difference?",o:["The insurer","The client","The lender","RIBO"],a:1,e:"The insurer pays actual cash value. The client still owes the remaining loan balance unless they have depreciation waiver or gap coverage."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks whether OPCF 43 applies to a 6-year-old used car. What's the MOST likely answer?",o:["Yes, always","No. Depreciation waiver is usually for new or nearly new vehicles within set time limits","Only for trucks","Only for leased cars"],a:1,e:"Removing depreciation deduction endorsements have eligibility rules based on vehicle age and the time since purchase."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's rental car (from an OPCF 20 claim) costs $60 a day, but the endorsement limit is $50 a day. Who pays the difference?",o:["The insurer","The client","The at-fault driver always","The broker"],a:1,e:"OPCF 20 pays up to its limits. Extra rental cost above the limit is the client's responsibility, unless it's recovered elsewhere."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the main benefit of OPCF 44R if your client is hit by an uninsured driver?",o:["It adds collision coverage","It can top up compensation above the uninsured automobile coverage, up to the insured's own liability limit, as its wording allows","It removes DCPD","It pays for rental cars"],a:1,e:"Family Protection responds to inadequately insured and uninsured motorists, up to the insured's liability limit, minus other amounts available."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks why accident benefits are called 'no-fault'. The BEST explanation is:",o:["No one is ever at fault","Your own insurer pays your accident benefits regardless of who caused the accident","The government pays","They only apply if you're not at fault"],a:1,e:"No-fault refers to who pays benefits, not to fault itself. Fault still matters for premiums and lawsuits."},
{d:"auto",q:"Under the post-July 2026 rules, who decides whether optional accident benefits such as income replacement are included?",o:["FSRA for each driver","The named insured, by choosing the optional coverage","The broker alone","The police"],a:1,e:"Optional benefits are chosen by the client. Brokers must explain them so the client makes an informed decision.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"A client earns $1,500 a week and wants more than the standard income replacement benefit, if they choose it. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing, because the standard benefit always covers income","Explain the optional higher weekly limits and document the client's choice","Tell them to buy life insurance","Add OPCF 28A"],a:1,e:"The standard income replacement is 70% of gross income up to $400 a week. Higher weekly limits are optional. Advice and the client's decision should be documented.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"Since July 1, 2026, auto insurance is the first payer for medical and rehabilitation expenses. What does 'first payer' mean?",o:["The auto insurer pays before the claimant's workplace health plan for those expenses","Workplace plans always pay first","OHIP pays first","The at-fault driver pays first"],a:0,e:"Under the reform, auto insurers pay medical and rehab benefits first, instead of after a claimant's extended health plan.",y:1},
{d:"auto",q:"A pedestrian with no car insurance is hit by your client's insured car. Where does the pedestrian usually claim accident benefits?",o:["The Motor Vehicle Accident Claims Fund always","The insurer of the car that hit them, if they have no other auto policy available to them","RIBO","OHIP only"],a:1,e:"The SABS priority rules send a claimant to their own or a family member's policy first. If none applies, the insurer of the vehicle that struck them pays."},
{d:"auto",q:"A passenger in your client's car has their own car insurance. Where do they usually claim accident benefits first?",o:["Your client's insurer","Their own auto insurer","The other driver's insurer","The Claims Fund"],a:1,e:"Priority rules generally send injured people to their own auto policy first, or the policy of someone they depend on."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is a 'catastrophic impairment' in Ontario auto insurance?",o:["Any injury over $3,500","A very serious injury meeting the SABS definition, such as severe brain injury or paraplegia","A broken arm","Any injury causing time off work"],a:1,e:"Catastrophic impairment is defined in the SABS. It unlocks the higher $1,000,000 combined medical, rehab and attendant care limit."},
{d:"auto",q:"Within how many days should an injured person usually notify their insurer that they intend to apply for accident benefits?",o:["7 days","30 days","1 year","2 years"],a:0,e:"The SABS sets short notice timelines, generally within 7 days of the accident, or as soon as practicable. Late notice has to be explained."},
{d:"auto",q:"Which statement about suing an at-fault driver for injuries in Ontario is correct?",o:["You can never sue","You can sue for pain and suffering only if the injury meets a legal threshold, and deductibles can apply","You can always sue for any amount","Only the insurer can sue"],a:1,e:"Ontario limits lawsuits for pain and suffering to serious injuries (the threshold), and statutory deductibles apply to some awards."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car is damaged by an identified uninsured driver. Which coverage helps with the car damage?",o:["Section 5, uninsured automobile, within its limits and conditions","OPCF 20","Accident benefits","Section 3"],a:0,e:"Uninsured automobile coverage covers damage to the insured car caused by an identified uninsured driver, within its limits."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks what the minimum limit is for uninsured automobile coverage. It is generally:",o:["$50,000","$200,000","$1,000,000","Unlimited"],a:1,e:"Uninsured automobile coverage generally matches the $200,000 minimum. OPCF 44R can add protection above it."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client has a G1 licence. What does a G1 driver need when driving?",o:["Nothing special","A fully licensed driver accompanying them, among other restrictions","Commercial insurance","A CVOR"],a:1,e:"Ontario's graduated licensing (G1, G2, G) places restrictions on new drivers. Insurers rate new drivers by licence level and experience."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks why driving experience matters in auto rating. Which is the BEST answer?",o:["It doesn't","Statistically, less experienced drivers have more claims, so insurers use experience as a rating factor","RIBO requires it","Only for commercial vehicles"],a:1,e:"Insurers use factors such as experience, driving record, vehicle and territory, within FSRA-approved rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client has a conviction for driving without due care. How does it usually affect insurance?",o:["No effect","It can raise premiums or affect eligibility for several years","It cancels the policy immediately","It only affects the licence"],a:1,e:"Convictions are rating factors and can affect eligibility under insurers' approved rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's licence is suspended for impaired driving. What should the broker do?",o:["Ignore it","Report it to the insurer as a material change. It affects coverage and eligibility","Remove the client from the policy without telling the insurer","Hide it until renewal"],a:1,e:"Suspensions and serious convictions are material. Driving while suspended can also affect coverage."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client wants to use their car to drive for a rideshare app. What does the broker tell them?",o:["Personal policies always cover it","They need rideshare coverage through their insurer or the platform's policy, because carrying paying passengers changes the risk","Only DCPD applies","No insurance is needed"],a:1,e:"Carrying passengers for compensation needs specific coverage. In Ontario this is often handled through endorsements or the platform's fleet policy."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's son takes the car to university in another province for 8 months. What should the broker check?",o:["Nothing","Whether the change in where the car is kept and who drives it needs to be reported, and whether the insurer allows it","Only the CGL","Whether to cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"The garaging location and the principal driver are rating factors. Insurers need to know about long-term changes."},
{d:"auto",q:"An Ontario resident moves permanently to Alberta. What happens with their Ontario auto policy?",o:["It continues forever","They must get insurance that meets Alberta's requirements and register the vehicle there within the required time","Ontario's OAP 1 applies in Alberta forever","RIBO transfers it"],a:1,e:"Auto insurance rules differ by province. When someone moves, they need a policy in the new province."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the effect of the OAP 1's statutory condition requiring the insured to notify the insurer 'promptly' of a material change?",o:["The insured can wait until renewal","Failing to report can affect coverage","It only applies to commercial vehicles","It's optional"],a:1,e:"Material changes must be reported promptly. Failing to do so can lead to denied claims or cancellation."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client cancels their auto policy mid-term. How is the refund usually calculated?",o:["Pro-rata always","Often on a short-rate basis (keeping a penalty), depending on the policy terms","No refund","Double the unused premium"],a:1,e:"When the insured cancels, the refund is often short-rate. When the insurer cancels, it's usually pro-rata."},
{d:"auto",q:"An insurer cancels a policy mid-term for a permitted reason. How is the refund usually calculated?",o:["Short-rate","Pro-rata, the full unused portion","No refund","50% of the premium"],a:1,e:"Insurer-initiated cancellations usually return premium pro-rata."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client buys a second car and wants it added. How is a newly acquired vehicle usually treated?",o:["Never covered until added","The OAP 1 gives limited automatic coverage for a newly acquired car if the insurer is notified within the required time","Covered forever without notice","Covered only under DCPD"],a:1,e:"The OAP 1 provides time-limited automatic coverage for newly acquired autos, subject to conditions. Notify the insurer right away."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client drives a friend's car occasionally. Is there any coverage under the client's own policy?",o:["None","The OAP 1 provides some coverage when driving other automobiles, subject to conditions, usually as excess","Full primary coverage","Only accident benefits"],a:1,e:"Driving other cars is covered in limited ways, usually as excess to the car owner's policy. Regular use of another car must be disclosed."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks if their OAP 1 covers personal belongings stolen from their car. What's the answer?",o:["Yes, under comprehensive","No. Belongings are usually covered under the client's home or tenant policy","Yes, under DCPD","Yes, under accident benefits"],a:1,e:"Auto policies cover the vehicle and its equipment, not personal belongings inside it."},
{d:"auto",q:"A motorcycle owner asks if accident benefits apply to motorcycles. What's correct?",o:["Motorcycles don't need insurance","Motorcycles are insured as automobiles and accident benefits apply, though rating differs","Only the passenger is covered","Only DCPD applies"],a:1,e:"Motorcycles need mandatory coverage including accident benefits. Premiums reflect their higher injury risk."},
{d:"auto",q:"A snowmobile is ridden on a public trail. What insurance is generally needed?",o:["None","Liability insurance (motor vehicle liability coverage) as required by Ontario law","Only a home policy","Only a CGL"],a:1,e:"Off-road vehicles, including snowmobiles, need liability insurance when operated off the owner's land in Ontario."},
{d:"auto",q:"The Ontario Motorist Protection Plan, introduced in 1990, is best known for:",o:["Creating RIBO","Bringing in no-fault accident benefits together with limits on suing for injuries","Creating the Facility Association","Making DCPD optional"],a:1,e:"The 1990 Ontario Motorist Protection Plan reshaped auto insurance with no-fault benefits and a threshold on injury lawsuits. Later reforms built on it."},
{d:"auto",q:"A driver with several at-fault accidents can't find any regular insurer. Where can they get coverage?",o:["Nowhere","Facility Association, through a broker or agent","RIBO","FSRA directly"],a:1,e:"Facility Association is the residual market for auto, usually at higher premiums."},
{d:"auto",q:"What is the purpose of a pink liability card?",o:["To prove the vehicle is insured, as required by law","To report claims","To pay premiums","To register with RIBO"],a:0,e:"Drivers must carry proof of insurance and show it on request. Electronic proof is accepted where offered."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks if a minor fender-bender with $1,500 of damage must be reported to police. Under the Highway Traffic Act threshold:",o:["Yes, always","No, if no one is injured and the combined damage appears to be $2,000 or less, police reporting isn't required","Yes, within 1 hour","Only if the other driver insists"],a:1,e:"Police reporting is required when there's injury or damage appears to exceed $2,000. Clients should still tell their insurer as the policy requires."},
{d:"auto",q:"Your client is in an accident and the other driver says, 'Let's not involve insurance.' Two weeks later the other driver claims injuries. What's the risk?",o:["None","Late reporting can breach the policy's notice conditions and complicate the defence","The other driver loses all rights","DCPD pays everything"],a:1,e:"Clients should report accidents to their insurer promptly as the policy requires, even if they plan to pay small damage themselves."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client hits a parked car and leaves a note. The damage is minor. What should the client do with their insurer?",o:["Nothing","Report the accident to their insurer as required by the policy","Pay cash and never tell anyone","Report only if sued"],a:1,e:"Policies require reporting accidents. The client may choose to pay small damages, but the insurer should know about the incident."},
{d:"auto",q:"Which factor generally does NOT affect Ontario auto premiums?",o:["Driving record","Territory (where the car is kept)","The vehicle's make and model","The broker's commission level"],a:3,e:"Premiums are set by the insurer's approved rates and rating factors. Brokers don't set rates."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client has a conviction for distracted driving (using a phone). How is it usually treated?",o:["Ignored by insurers","As a serious conviction that can significantly raise premiums","As a minor parking ticket","As a DCPD claim"],a:1,e:"Distracted driving convictions carry heavy penalties and are treated seriously in rating."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks whether their OAP 1 pays for a tow and storage after a covered collision. Generally:",o:["Never","Towing and storage connected to a covered loss can be part of the claim, within the policy's terms","Only under accident benefits","Only with OPCF 28A"],a:1,e:"Reasonable towing and storage tied to a covered physical damage loss are usually payable. Clients should avoid long storage charges."},
{d:"auto",q:"After a collision, a client is told by a tow operator to go to a specific body shop. What should the broker advise?",o:["Always go where the tow truck says","The client can choose their own repair shop, and should call the insurer before agreeing to anything","The insurer must pay any shop","Only dealer shops are allowed"],a:1,e:"Ontario has rules to protect drivers from tow and storage abuses. Clients choose their repair shop and should contact the insurer."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client claims their car was stolen, but the insurer suspects the client arranged it. If fraud is proven:",o:["The claim is still paid","The claim can be denied, the policy voided, and the client may face criminal charges","Only the deductible doubles","The broker pays"],a:1,e:"Fraud defeats coverage and can lead to prosecution. Brokers must not help with false claims."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client has a 'conviction protector' endorsement. What does it do?",o:["Removes convictions from the driving record","Prevents a first minor conviction from affecting the premium, under the insurer's rules","Pays traffic fines","Excludes a driver"],a:1,e:"Conviction protection protects the premium from a first minor conviction. It doesn't change the MTO driving record."},
{d:"auto",q:"A family adds their 16-year-old with a G2 licence to the policy. What's the MOST likely effect?",o:["The premium goes down","The premium usually rises because inexperienced drivers have higher risk","No change","The policy is cancelled"],a:1,e:"New drivers increase risk. Insurers may offer discounts for driver training or good grades."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks why their premium dropped when they completed a driver education course. The reason is:",o:["RIBO subsidizes it","Many insurers give discounts for approved driver training because it reduces claims","It's required by law","DCPD was removed"],a:1,e:"Approved driver training courses can earn discounts under insurers' rating programs."},
{d:"auto",q:"What does the term 'listed driver' mean in an auto policy?",o:["Any driver who ever drives the car","A driver named on the policy as a regular or occasional driver","A driver excluded from coverage","A passenger"],a:1,e:"Listed drivers are named on the policy. Since July 2026 this also affects who can claim optional accident benefits."},
{d:"auto",q:"A business owner uses a personal car for business errands occasionally. What should the broker check?",o:["Nothing","That the use is properly rated (for example business use) and disclosed","That a CGL is added","That OPCF 28A is added"],a:1,e:"Vehicle use is a rating factor. Business use must be disclosed so the policy is properly rated."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks about a 'specialty purpose' vehicle, such as a motorhome. Is it insured on the OAP 1?",o:["No, never","Yes, with appropriate rating and possibly additional endorsements for its special use","Only under a home policy","Only by Facility Association"],a:1,e:"Motorhomes, antique cars and other specialty vehicles are insured on the OAP 1, often with special rating and endorsements."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks if their auto insurance covers them while driving in Mexico. The OAP 1:",o:["Covers Mexico fully","Does not cover driving in Mexico, so local Mexican insurance is needed","Covers Mexico only for liability","Covers Mexico with OPCF 27"],a:1,e:"The OAP 1 covers Canada, the U.S. and certain vessels. Mexico requires Mexican insurance."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client asks what happens if they don't pay their auto premium installment. Generally:",o:["Nothing","The insurer can cancel the policy for non-payment after proper notice","RIBO pays it","The broker pays it"],a:1,e:"Non-payment is a valid reason for cancellation after the notice required by the statutory conditions."},
{d:"auto",q:"After an accident, the client admits fault to the other driver at the scene. Under the statutory conditions:",o:["That's required","The insured should not voluntarily assume liability, and admissions can complicate the claim","It automatically makes them 100% at fault for DCPD","It cancels the policy"],a:1,e:"Insureds shouldn't admit liability or settle. Fault for DCPD is still decided under the Fault Determination Rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"A client's car is damaged in a car wash. Which coverage MOST likely applies?",o:["DCPD","Collision or comprehensive depending on how it happened, or a claim against the car wash","Accident benefits","Section 5"],a:1,e:"Damage at a business may be the business's liability. The client's own physical damage coverage can also respond."},
{d:"auto",q:"A fault chart (Fault Determination Rules diagram) shows that both cars changed lanes into the same lane at the same time. The fault split is usually:",o:["100% / 0%","50% / 50%","25% / 75%","Decided by the drivers"],a:1,e:"When both vehicles change lanes into each other at the same time, each is generally 50% at fault."},
{d:"auto",q:"Car A is making a left turn at an intersection and collides with Car B, which is going straight through from the opposite direction. Under the Fault Determination Rules, the turning car is usually:",o:["0% at fault","100% at fault","50% at fault","25% at fault"],a:1,e:"A left-turning vehicle that collides with an oncoming vehicle going straight is generally 100% at fault."},
{d:"auto",q:"Car A is backing out of a driveway and hits Car B travelling on the road. Who is usually at fault?",o:["Car B","Car A, the vehicle leaving the driveway or parking spot","Both 50%","No one"],a:1,e:"A vehicle entering the road from a driveway or parking spot is generally at fault for colliding with traffic already on the road."},
{d:"auto",q:"Car A runs a stop sign and hits Car B, which had no stop sign. Who is at fault?",o:["Car B","Car A, 100%","50% each","No one"],a:1,e:"The driver who fails to obey a stop sign is generally 100% at fault under the Fault Determination Rules."},
{d:"auto",q:"A pile-up involves four cars rear-ending each other in a chain. How do the Fault Determination Rules generally treat chain reactions?",o:["Everyone is 50%","Each rear-ending vehicle is generally at fault for hitting the vehicle in front of it, under the specific chain reaction rules","Only the first car is at fault","Police decide"],a:1,e:"Chain reaction collisions have specific rules that assign fault for each impact."},
// SET 3 — HABITATIONAL
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's detached garage is destroyed by fire. The Coverage B limit is often set as a percentage of:",o:["Coverage A","Coverage E","The deductible","The mortgage"],a:0,e:"Many wordings set Coverage B (detached structures) as a percentage of the dwelling limit, such as 10%, with options to increase it."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's attached deck is damaged by a falling tree. Which coverage applies?",o:["Coverage A, Dwelling, because the deck is attached","Coverage B","Coverage C","Coverage E"],a:0,e:"Structures attached to the dwelling are part of Coverage A. Detached structures fall under Coverage B."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's fence is damaged by a windstorm. Which coverage usually applies?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage B, detached private structures","Coverage C","Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Fences are generally treated as detached private structures under Coverage B."},
{d:"hab",q:"A fire damages a rented home. The tenant's belongings are destroyed. Which policy pays for the tenant's belongings?",o:["The landlord's policy","The tenant's own policy (Coverage C)","The city","No policy"],a:1,e:"Landlords insure the building and their own property. Tenants must insure their own contents."},
{d:"hab",q:"A landlord rents out a house they own but don't live in. Which type of policy is usually needed?",o:["A standard homeowners policy","A rented dwelling or landlord policy","A tenant's policy","A condo unit owner's policy"],a:1,e:"Owner-occupied homeowners forms don't fit rental properties. Landlords need rented dwelling coverage, including loss of rental income."},
{d:"hab",q:"A rental house becomes unlivable after a fire, and the landlord loses rent for 4 months. Which coverage helps the landlord?",o:["Additional living expense","Fair rental value (loss of rental income)","Coverage F","Coverage E"],a:1,e:"Fair rental value or loss of rents pays the landlord's lost rental income while the property is repaired."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's guest is injured when the homeowner's dog knocks them down. The guest sues. Which coverage defends the homeowner?",o:["Coverage C","Coverage E, legal liability, which also pays defence costs","Coverage D","Coverage B"],a:1,e:"Liability coverage pays damages the insured is legally liable for and the costs of defending the lawsuit."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's son accidentally breaks a neighbour's window playing baseball. The homeowner wants to pay for it without arguing about fault. Which coverage fits?",o:["Coverage E only after a lawsuit","Coverage G, voluntary payment for damage to property","Coverage A","Coverage H"],a:1,e:"Coverage G pays for damage to others' property regardless of liability, often up to a small limit."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner hires a nanny who is injured while working in the home. Which coverage may respond?",o:["Coverage H, voluntary compensation for residence employees","Coverage C","Coverage B","Coverage D"],a:0,e:"Coverage H can pay benefits to residence employees injured at work, regardless of fault."},
{d:"hab",q:"Which is usually covered under a homeowners policy's personal liability?",o:["Injuries caused while driving the family car","Accidental injury to a neighbour caused by the insured's activities","Injuries to the insured's own children","Business liability from a home salon"],a:1,e:"Personal liability covers accidental harm to others. Auto, business and injuries to household members are excluded."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's son drives a golf cart on a public road and injures a pedestrian. Will the homeowners policy cover it?",o:["Yes, always","Probably not. Homeowners liability excludes most motorized vehicles used off the premises","Only under Coverage C","Only under Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Motorized vehicles are mostly excluded from homeowners liability, except for limited items like lawn mowers on the premises."},
{d:"hab",q:"What is the 'insuring agreement' in a home policy?",o:["The list of exclusions","The section stating what the insurer agrees to cover","The premium invoice","The broker's licence"],a:1,e:"The insuring agreement sets out what the policy covers. Exclusions, conditions and definitions then narrow or explain it."},
{d:"hab",q:"Where in a policy would you look to see whether wear and tear is covered?",o:["The declarations page","The exclusions section","The broker's notes","The billing schedule"],a:1,e:"Exclusions list what isn't covered. Brokers should know how to find exclusions in any wording."},
{d:"hab",q:"What does the declarations page of a home policy show?",o:["The full wording","Key details: the named insured, address, coverages, limits, deductible and premium","Only exclusions","RIBO rules"],a:1,e:"The declarations (dec) page summarizes the specific policy. The wording explains what the coverages mean."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's basement floods because a washing machine hose bursts suddenly. Is it usually covered?",o:["Yes, as sudden and accidental escape of water from a plumbing appliance","No, water is never covered","Only with overland water","Only with earthquake"],a:0,e:"Sudden escape of water from plumbing or appliances is generally covered. Gradual leaks, sewer backup and overland water are different."},
{d:"hab",q:"Water seeps into a basement through cracks in the foundation because of high ground water. Without a ground water endorsement, this is usually:",o:["Covered","Excluded","Covered under Coverage D","Covered under Coverage F"],a:1,e:"Seepage through foundations from ground water is a standard exclusion, unless a ground water endorsement is added."},
{d:"hab",q:"Ice builds up on a roof in winter, and melting water backs up under the shingles into the house. This is called:",o:["Ice damming, which may be covered or excluded depending on the wording","Overland water","Sewer backup","Earthquake"],a:0,e:"Ice damming water damage depends on the wording. Some policies cover it, others limit or exclude it."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client lives in a high flood-risk zone, and the insurer won't offer overland water coverage. What should the broker do?",o:["Hide it from the client","Explain the gap clearly, check other markets, and document the advice and the client's decision","Promise coverage anyway","Add sewer backup and say it covers floods"],a:1,e:"Brokers must explain uninsurable or unavailable exposures, look for options and document everything."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner installs a backwater valve and a sump pump with a battery backup. How might this affect their insurance?",o:["No effect","Insurers may offer better water coverage or discounts because the risk is lower","It voids the policy","It removes Coverage E"],a:1,e:"Loss prevention devices can improve eligibility and pricing for water coverages."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's $8,000 mountain bike is stolen. The policy has a special limit for bicycles. What could have prevented the shortfall?",o:["Scheduling the bike for its full value","Adding OPCF 27","Lowering Coverage A","Adding overland water"],a:0,e:"Scheduling insures high-value items for their appraised or purchase value, usually on broader terms."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner keeps $5,000 in cash at home, and it's stolen. Under most policies:",o:["Fully covered","Covered only up to a low special limit for money","Not a theft","Covered under Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Money, bullion and similar items have low special limits. Clients shouldn't keep large amounts of cash at home."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's art collection is worth $60,000. What is the BEST way to insure it?",o:["Rely on Coverage C","Schedule it on a valuable articles floater or endorsement, based on appraisals","Buy a CGL","Add OPCF 43"],a:1,e:"Fine art has special limits and valuation issues. Scheduled coverage with appraisals is best."},
{d:"hab",q:"What does 'replacement cost' mean for contents?",o:["The item's value after depreciation","The cost to replace the item with new property of similar kind and quality, without deducting depreciation","The original purchase price","The insurer's choice"],a:1,e:"Replacement cost pays the cost of a new comparable item, usually once it's actually replaced."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner chooses not to rebuild after a total loss and moves elsewhere. Under many replacement cost wordings, the insurer pays:",o:["Full replacement cost","Actual cash value, or a settlement limited as the wording requires","Nothing","Market value of the land"],a:1,e:"Replacement cost usually requires repair or replacement. Not rebuilding often limits the payout to actual cash value or a reduced amount."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's roof is 25 years old and leaks. Rain damages the ceiling. Which is MOST likely?",o:["Fully covered","The roof repair itself is wear and tear, and resulting damage depends on the wording","Covered under sewer backup","Covered under Coverage F"],a:1,e:"Wear and tear isn't covered. Resulting damage may or may not be covered depending on the wording, so read the exclusions."},
{d:"hab",q:"An insurer asks for the age of the roof, wiring, plumbing and heating system. Why?",o:["To set Coverage E limits","These affect the likelihood of fire and water losses, so they're key underwriting factors","RIBO requires it","For marketing"],a:1,e:"The age and condition of major systems affect risk. Old wiring like knob-and-tube or aluminum can affect eligibility."},
{d:"hab",q:"A home has aluminum wiring. Why might an insurer care?",o:["It's cheaper","It carries a higher fire risk if not properly maintained or remediated, so insurers may require an inspection","It's a security device","It reduces premiums"],a:1,e:"Older wiring types, like aluminum and knob-and-tube, can raise fire risk. Insurers often require an electrician's inspection."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner runs a daycare for 5 children at home for pay. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing","Treat it as a material business exposure and arrange appropriate coverage, often a specific endorsement or policy","Add sewer backup","Add OPCF 20"],a:1,e:"Home daycares create significant liability exposure that homeowners policies exclude or limit."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner rents the basement apartment to a tenant. Which statements are true?",o:["Nothing changes","It must be disclosed. It changes occupancy, liability exposure and possibly the policy form","Only Coverage C increases","The tenant is covered under the owner's policy"],a:1,e:"Renting part of a home changes the risk. The tenant needs their own tenant's policy."},
{d:"hab",q:"A condo unit owner's policy has 'unit improvements and betterments' coverage. Which item is covered by it?",o:["The building's roof","Upgraded kitchen cabinets the owner installed","The lobby furniture","The parking garage"],a:1,e:"Improvements made by the owner beyond the standard unit are covered by the owner's betterments coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"Water leaks from a condo owner's unit and damages the unit below. The condo corporation's deductible is $25,000, and its by-laws make the responsible owner pay the deductible. Which coverage helps the owner?",o:["Deductible assessment / loss assessment coverage on the unit owner's policy","Coverage F","OPCF 44R","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Unit owner policies can cover the owner's share of, or responsibility for, the corporation's deductible."},
{d:"hab",q:"A condo owner's unit is unlivable for 3 months after a fire in the building. Which coverage helps?",o:["Coverage D, additional living expense on the unit owner's policy","The corporation's CGL","Coverage B","Coverage H"],a:0,e:"Additional living expense on the owner's policy pays extra living costs while the unit is repaired."},
{d:"hab",q:"What is a condominium's 'standard unit' definition used for?",o:["Setting the owner's mortgage","Showing which finishes the corporation insures and which the owner must insure","Calculating property taxes","Defining RIBO jurisdiction"],a:1,e:"The standard unit definition, set out in the corporation's by-laws, decides the line between the corporation's and the owner's coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A tenant leaves a pot on the stove, and the fire damages the landlord's building. The landlord's insurer pays and then pursues the tenant. What protects the tenant?",o:["Nothing","The liability coverage (including tenant's legal liability) on the tenant's policy","The landlord's policy","Coverage B"],a:1,e:"The landlord's insurer can subrogate against a negligent tenant. The tenant's liability coverage responds."},
{d:"hab",q:"A student lives in a university residence. Their belongings may be covered under:",o:["Their parents' home policy, often with a limit for students away at school","No policy","The university's CGL","An OPCF 20"],a:0,e:"Many home policies cover dependent students' belongings away at school, usually with a limit. Check the wording."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner leaves on a 3-week winter vacation. What's the BEST advice?",o:["Turn off the heat","Arrange for someone to check the home as the policy requires, or shut off and drain the water","Do nothing","Cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"Freezing exclusions are common when homes are left unheated or unchecked. Clients should follow the policy's conditions."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's house is vacant for 2 months while it's being sold. Under most policies, what happens?",o:["Nothing","Coverage for certain perils may be suspended after the vacancy period, so the insurer should be told and a vacancy permit or policy arranged","Coverage doubles","The policy becomes a tenant form"],a:1,e:"Vacancy restrictions are common. Brokers should tell clients and arrange appropriate coverage for vacant homes."},
{d:"hab",q:"What is the difference between 'vacant' and 'unoccupied' in home insurance?",o:["They mean exactly the same","Vacant usually means no people and no contents, while unoccupied means contents remain but nobody is living there, depending on definitions","Vacant only applies to condos","Unoccupied only applies to businesses"],a:1,e:"Definitions vary by wording, but vacancy usually means empty of furnishings too. Both can trigger restrictions."},
{d:"hab",q:"A seasonal cottage is used only in summer. Which is MOST likely true?",o:["It has the same coverage as a main home","Seasonal policies may have different perils, theft conditions and winter requirements","It can't be insured","It's covered under the main home's Coverage B"],a:1,e:"Seasonal or secondary dwelling policies often have narrower coverage and specific conditions."},
{d:"hab",q:"A cottage owner rents the cottage to vacationers for several weeks each summer. What should the broker do?",o:["Nothing","Disclose the rental use to the insurer and arrange coverage that allows it","Add DCPD","Cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"Rental use is material. The policy may need to allow rental or be replaced with a suitable form."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's trailer is stored in the driveway, and a storm damages it. Under most home policies:",o:["Fully covered as contents","Coverage for trailers may be limited or excluded, so separate coverage or an endorsement may be needed","Covered under Coverage A","Covered under Coverage F"],a:1,e:"Trailers and recreational vehicles often have limited coverage on home policies. Check the wording and consider separate coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's small outboard motorboat under the policy's horsepower limit is stolen from the cottage. Which is MOST likely?",o:["Not covered","Covered under the home policy within any special limits for watercraft","Covered under OAP 1","Covered under CGL"],a:1,e:"Small watercraft are often covered within limits on home policies. Larger ones need separate watercraft coverage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's credit card is stolen and used fraudulently. Which coverage may help?",o:["Credit card and forgery coverage, often included or available on home policies","Coverage B","Coverage H","OPCF 20"],a:0,e:"Home policies often include limited coverage for credit card fraud, forgery and counterfeit money."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's food spoils after a power outage. Is it covered?",o:["Always fully","Sometimes. Some policies include limited food spoilage coverage, depending on the cause and wording","Never","Only under OAP 1"],a:1,e:"Food spoilage coverage, if included, usually has a limit and conditions about the cause of the outage."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's tree falls onto the neighbour's house during a windstorm, though the tree was healthy. Generally, who pays for the neighbour's house?",o:["The homeowner's liability coverage always","The neighbour's own property insurance, since the homeowner wasn't negligent","RIBO","No one"],a:1,e:"Liability depends on negligence. A healthy tree falling in a storm is usually the neighbour's own property claim."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner knew their dead tree was dangerous but didn't remove it, and it falls on the neighbour's car. What's MOST likely?",o:["The neighbour's insurer pays and may subrogate against the homeowner, whose liability coverage may respond","No one is responsible","Only the city pays","Coverage B pays the neighbour"],a:0,e:"Knowing about a hazard and ignoring it can make the owner negligent, so their liability coverage may respond."},
{d:"hab",q:"What is the 'insured location' or 'premises' in a home policy used for?",o:["Setting the broker's commission","Deciding where some coverages apply, such as the dwelling and premises liability","Setting the auto rating territory","Defining RIBO's jurisdiction"],a:1,e:"Many coverages depend on the described premises. Changes in location must be reported."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client buys a new house and closes the deal next week. When should home insurance start?",o:["After moving in","No later than the date ownership transfers (closing), and lenders usually require proof before closing","When the first claim happens","After 30 days"],a:1,e:"Coverage should be in force from the date the buyer takes on the risk. Lenders require proof of insurance."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client sells their home. When should the policy be cancelled?",o:["Before the sale closes","After the sale closes, when the client no longer has an insurable interest","Never","A year later"],a:1,e:"Coverage should stay in place until ownership transfers. Cancelling early leaves a gap."},
{d:"hab",q:"An insurer offers a claims-free discount. Why do insurers reward claim-free clients?",o:["RIBO requires it","Past claims history predicts future claims","To pay brokers less","It's required by law"],a:1,e:"Claims history is a strong rating factor. Frequent small claims can affect pricing or eligibility."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client wants to claim a $600 loss on a policy with a $500 deductible. What should the broker discuss?",o:["Always claim","That a small claim pays only $100 and may affect future premiums or eligibility, so the client can decide","Never claim","That RIBO will reimburse"],a:1,e:"Brokers should explain the practical effect of small claims. The decision belongs to the client."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's home has a monitored alarm system. How does this usually affect the policy?",o:["No effect","It may earn a discount because it reduces theft and fire risk","It voids the policy","It removes the deductible"],a:1,e:"Monitored alarms reduce losses, and insurers often reward them with discounts."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's house is located far from a fire hall and fire hydrant. How might this affect insurance?",o:["No effect","Premiums may be higher because fire protection affects the severity of fire losses","It lowers the premium","It cancels the policy"],a:1,e:"Distance to fire protection and hydrants is a rating factor for fire risk."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner wants to insure their house for less than its rebuild cost to save money. What's the main risk?",o:["None","Underinsurance: a large loss may exceed the limit, and some policies apply co-insurance penalties","Higher deductible","Coverage E increases"],a:1,e:"Insuring below rebuild cost risks not having enough to rebuild, and losing guaranteed replacement provisions."},
{d:"hab",q:"A client's home office has $15,000 of computer equipment used for their job as an employee. Is it covered as personal contents?",o:["Always","It depends. Business property may be limited even if the client is an employee, so check the wording","Never","Only under OAP 1"],a:1,e:"Home policies often limit business property. The client may need to raise limits or add an endorsement."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowner's son, age 22, lives at home. Is he usually an insured under the parents' home policy?",o:["No","Often yes, if he's a household resident related to the named insured, as the definitions allow","Only for liability","Only if he pays premium"],a:1,e:"Most wordings include resident relatives as insureds. Definitions vary, so check them."},
{d:"hab",q:"A homeowners policy contains a statutory condition about 'misrepresentation'. What does it mean?",o:["The insurer must pay any claim","False statements or omissions of material facts can void coverage","Only brokers can misrepresent","It only applies to auto"],a:1,e:"Property statutory conditions under the Insurance Act include misrepresentation, notice and proof of loss requirements."},
{d:"hab",q:"After a property loss, what are the insured's main duties under the statutory conditions?",o:["Wait for the insurer to do everything","Notify the insurer promptly, protect the property from further damage, and provide proof of loss","Admit fault","Throw away damaged items immediately"],a:1,e:"The insured must give notice, mitigate (protect) and prove the loss. Damaged items should be kept until the adjuster sees them."},
{d:"hab",q:"Under the property statutory conditions, if the insured and insurer disagree about the value of a loss, what process is available?",o:["Ask RIBO","Appraisal, where each side appoints an appraiser and an umpire decides disagreements","Court only","No process"],a:1,e:"The appraisal process resolves disputes about the amount of loss, not whether there's coverage."},
// SET 3 — COMMERCIAL
{d:"com",q:"A bakery owner asks why their commercial property policy lists building, equipment and stock separately. The main reason is:",o:["Each is a different type of property with its own limit and valuation","RIBO requires three separate policies","Only stock can be insured for replacement cost","Equipment is never covered"],a:0,e:"Commercial property forms set separate limits for the building, equipment (contents) and stock so each is insured to its proper value."},
{d:"com",q:"A retailer's stock rises sharply every November and December for the holidays. What helps keep the stock limit adequate during that peak?",o:["A seasonal or peak season increase in the stock limit","Lowering the deductible","Builders' risk","An occurrence CGL"],a:0,e:"Seasonal stock (peak season) provisions or reporting forms raise the stock limit for busy periods so the business isn't underinsured when stock is highest."},
{d:"com",q:"A business carries $500,000 on its building, which is worth $1,000,000, under an 80% co-insurance clause. A $100,000 loss occurs. Ignoring the deductible, what does the insurer pay?",o:["$100,000","$62,500","$50,000","$80,000"],a:1,e:"Required = 80% × $1,000,000 = $800,000. Payment = 500 ÷ 800 × $100,000 = $62,500."},
{d:"com",q:"A warehouse worth $2,000,000 is insured for $1,600,000 with an 80% co-insurance clause. A $300,000 loss occurs. Ignoring the deductible, the insurer pays:",o:["$240,000","$300,000","$1,600,000","$150,000"],a:1,e:"Required = 80% × $2,000,000 = $1,600,000. The insured carries exactly the required amount, so the full $300,000 is paid."},
{d:"com",q:"Why do commercial property policies have co-insurance clauses?",o:["To penalize insureds who choose a high deductible","To encourage insuring to full value so premiums are fair for everyone","To share losses with RIBO","To replace business interruption"],a:1,e:"Most losses are partial. Co-insurance stops businesses from buying low limits to save premium, which would be unfair to those who insure to value."},
{d:"com",q:"A business's building limit is based on what it would sell for on the real estate market. What's the main problem?",o:["None, market value is the right basis","Market value includes land and location factors that have nothing to do with rebuild cost","Market value is always too high","Insurers can't insure buildings at market value"],a:1,e:"Insure buildings for replacement (rebuild) cost. Market value includes land and can be far above or below the cost to rebuild."},
{d:"com",q:"What is a 'stated amount' or agreed amount provision on a commercial property policy designed to do?",o:["Remove the deductible","Suspend the co-insurance clause when the insured provides an acceptable valuation","Cover floods automatically","Turn the policy into claims-made"],a:1,e:"An agreed or stated amount provision waives the co-insurance penalty for the policy term, usually after the insured files a statement of values."},
{d:"com",q:"A business wants one limit that applies across several buildings at different locations, instead of separate limits per building. This is called:",o:["Blanket coverage","Subscription coverage","Specified perils","Excess coverage"],a:0,e:"Blanket coverage applies one total limit across several locations or types of property, so the limit can respond where the loss happens."},
{d:"com",q:"A commercial property policy has a $5,000 deductible. A covered fire causes $4,000 of damage. The insurer pays:",o:["$4,000","$0","$1,000","$5,000"],a:1,e:"The loss is below the deductible, so the insured absorbs all of it."},
{d:"com",q:"Which statement about 'named perils' and 'all risks' commercial property forms is correct?",o:["Named perils covers more than all risks","All risks covers any cause of loss that isn't specifically excluded","All risks has no exclusions","Named perils is only for homes"],a:1,e:"All risks forms cover everything except listed exclusions. Named perils forms only cover the perils listed. The burden of proof differs too."},
{d:"com",q:"Under an all risks commercial property policy, which loss is MOST likely excluded?",o:["A fire in the stockroom","Wear and tear on an old roof","A sudden windstorm","Theft by burglars"],a:1,e:"Wear and tear, gradual deterioration, rust and rot are standard exclusions because they aren't sudden, accidental events."},
{d:"com",q:"A manufacturer's business interruption policy has a 12-month indemnity period. What does that mean?",o:["Claims must be filed within 12 months","Income loss is covered for up to 12 months while the business is restored","The policy lasts 12 months","The deductible is 12 days"],a:1,e:"The indemnity period is the maximum time the policy pays income loss after damage. It should be long enough to fully rebuild and get customers back."},
{d:"com",q:"A business interruption claim follows a fire. Which expense would the insurer normally NOT pay?",o:["Rent that continues during the shutdown","Wages of key staff kept on payroll","The lost profit the business would have earned","Expenses that stop because the business is closed"],a:3,e:"BI pays lost profit and continuing expenses. Expenses that stop during the closure, such as some utilities or raw materials, aren't a loss."},
{d:"com",q:"A gift shop wants to know how much business interruption coverage to buy. What information does the broker need MOST?",o:["The owner's personal income tax","Financial statements showing revenue, expenses and profit","The building's age only","The number of parking spaces"],a:1,e:"BI limits are based on the business's earnings and continuing expenses, which come from its financial statements and projections."},
{d:"com",q:"A business closes for two weeks because police block the area after a fire at a neighbouring building. The business has no damage. Which BI extension could respond?",o:["Civil authority","Contingent business interruption","Equipment breakdown","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Civil authority extensions pay income loss when a government order prohibits access because of insured damage nearby, usually for a limited time."},
{d:"com",q:"A dental office's key staff must be paid even while the office is closed after a fire, to stop them from leaving. Which part of BI coverage addresses this?",o:["Ordinary payroll or continuing expenses","Tenants' legal liability","Crime","Products-completed operations"],a:0,e:"BI covers continuing expenses, which can include payroll, so the business can keep key people during repairs."},
{d:"com",q:"A broker suggests 'extra expense' coverage instead of standard business interruption for a newspaper. Why?",o:["Newspapers can't buy BI","The newspaper must keep publishing at any cost, so the extra cost of continuing matters more than lost income","Extra expense is cheaper for all businesses","Extra expense covers liability"],a:1,e:"Businesses that can't stop, like newspapers, hospitals and clinics, need extra expense to pay the added cost of staying open."},
{d:"com",q:"An equipment breakdown policy usually covers which of these?",o:["A boiler explosion or a sudden electrical failure in machinery","Wear and tear on old machines","Theft of equipment","Flood damage"],a:0,e:"Equipment breakdown, the old boiler and machinery coverage, covers sudden and accidental breakdown, including boiler explosions and electrical arcing."},
{d:"com",q:"Why do some jurisdictions and insurers require inspections of boilers and pressure vessels on equipment breakdown policies?",o:["To set the CGL limit","Because the breakdown insurer often inspects equipment to prevent losses","RIBO requires it","To value stock"],a:1,e:"Equipment breakdown insurers are known for loss prevention inspections. Regular inspection reduces explosions and breakdowns."},
{d:"com",q:"A store's cashier is robbed at gunpoint inside the premises. Which crime coverage applies?",o:["Money and securities inside the premises (robbery)","Employee dishonesty","CGL Coverage B","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Money and securities coverage protects cash taken by robbery, burglary or theft on the premises (and, with an extension, outside it)."},
{d:"com",q:"A store manager is robbed while carrying the day's cash to the bank. Which coverage applies?",o:["Money and securities outside the premises (messenger coverage)","Building coverage","Tenants' legal liability","Business interruption"],a:0,e:"Crime policies extend money and securities coverage to a messenger carrying funds outside the premises."},
{d:"com",q:"A fraudster tricks an accounts clerk into wiring $30,000 to a fake supplier account by email. Which coverage is designed for this kind of loss?",o:["Social engineering fraud or funds transfer fraud coverage under a crime or cyber policy","CGL Coverage A","Builders' risk","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Social engineering fraud coverage, sold as part of crime or cyber policies, responds when an employee is deceived into sending money. Standard employee dishonesty doesn't cover it, because the employee wasn't dishonest."},
{d:"com",q:"Which is the BEST way for a business to reduce employee dishonesty losses?",o:["Have one person handle all banking alone","Separate duties so no one person controls a whole money process, plus audits","Pay employees in cash","Raise the CGL limit"],a:1,e:"Separation of duties, reconciliations and audits are standard crime loss controls. Insurers ask about them."},
{d:"com",q:"A coffee shop customer burns their hand on spilled coffee served too hot and sues. Which coverage responds?",o:["CGL Coverage A, bodily injury","Crime","Business interruption","D&O"],a:0,e:"Bodily injury to a customer caused by the business's operations or products falls under CGL Coverage A."},
{d:"com",q:"A painting contractor accidentally splashes paint on a client's expensive car parked nearby. Which coverage responds?",o:["CGL Coverage A, property damage","The contractor's equipment floater","Builders' risk","E&O"],a:0,e:"Damage to third-party property caused by the insured's operations is CGL property damage."},
{d:"com",q:"A competitor sues a retailer for copying its slogan in an advertisement. Which part of the CGL may respond?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage B, personal and advertising injury","Coverage C","Coverage D"],a:1,e:"Coverage B covers listed advertising offences, which can include using another's advertising idea or infringing a slogan in an advertisement."},
{d:"com",q:"A business publishes a social media post falsely accusing a former supplier of fraud. The supplier sues for defamation. Which CGL coverage applies?",o:["Coverage A","Coverage B, personal and advertising injury","Coverage C","Products-completed operations"],a:1,e:"Defamation, meaning libel or slander, is a personal injury offence under Coverage B."},
{d:"com",q:"Why does a CGL exclude 'expected or intended' injury?",o:["Insurance covers accidents, not harm the insured meant to cause","It's a privacy rule","It only applies to cars","RIBO requires it"],a:0,e:"Liability insurance covers accidental harm. Deliberate harm isn't insurable and would encourage bad behaviour."},
{d:"com",q:"A renovation contractor's own poor workmanship requires the bathroom it built to be torn out and redone. No other property is damaged. The CGL usually:",o:["Pays to redo the work","Excludes the cost of fixing the insured's own faulty work","Pays only half","Pays under Coverage B"],a:1,e:"CGL policies exclude damage to 'your work' itself. They cover resulting damage to other property, such as a flooded floor below."},
{d:"com",q:"A food manufacturer must recall thousands of jars because of possible contamination. No one has been hurt yet. Does a standard CGL pay the recall costs?",o:["Yes, under Coverage A","No. Recall costs are excluded and need product recall coverage","Yes, under Coverage C","Only if a regulator orders it"],a:1,e:"The CGL excludes the cost of recalling products. Product recall insurance is a separate specialty coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A dry cleaner damages customers' clothing while cleaning it. Why might the CGL NOT respond?",o:["The clothing is in the insured's care, custody or control, which the CGL excludes","Clothing isn't property","Dry cleaners can't buy CGL","Only D&O covers it"],a:0,e:"The CGL excludes property in the insured's care, custody or control. Businesses holding customers' goods need a bailee or customers' goods coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A business signs a lease agreeing to take responsibility for the landlord's legal liability for injuries on the premises. In a CGL, this is known as:",o:["An insured contract or contractual liability","Advertising injury","An exclusion that can never be covered","A surety bond"],a:0,e:"CGLs generally cover liability assumed under an 'insured contract', such as a lease, within the policy's definitions."},
{d:"com",q:"A manufacturer's machine guard fails, and a factory worker employed by the manufacturer is injured. In Ontario, the worker's injury is usually handled by:",o:["The manufacturer's CGL","The Workplace Safety and Insurance Board (WSIB) system","The worker's home insurance","Equipment breakdown"],a:1,e:"Workplace injuries to employees in Ontario are generally covered by the WSIB system. The CGL excludes injury to the insured's own employees."},
{d:"com",q:"A landlord owns a small strip mall and doesn't operate a business in it. Which liability coverage does the landlord need?",o:["None","CGL coverage for premises liability as the property owner","Only crime coverage","Only D&O"],a:1,e:"Owners face premises liability claims, like slip-and-falls in common areas, even if they don't run a business there."},
{d:"com",q:"What does 'aggregate limit' mean on a CGL?",o:["The most paid for any one occurrence","The most paid for all claims combined during the policy period","The deductible","The premium"],a:1,e:"The per occurrence limit caps one event. The aggregate limit caps the total of all claims in the policy period."},
{d:"com",q:"A CGL has a $2,000,000 per occurrence limit and a $5,000,000 aggregate. Three separate claims of $2,000,000 each occur in one year. What's the most the insurer pays in total?",o:["$6,000,000","$5,000,000","$2,000,000","$4,000,000"],a:1,e:"Each claim is within the $2M occurrence limit, but the total is capped by the $5M aggregate."},
{d:"com",q:"An umbrella policy can 'drop down' in some cases. What does that mean?",o:["It replaces the deductible","It responds as primary coverage, above a self-insured retention, for some claims the primary policy doesn't cover","It cancels the primary policy","It covers only property"],a:1,e:"An umbrella may cover claims excluded by the primary policies, after a self-insured retention. An excess policy follows the primary terms only."},
{d:"com",q:"What is the main difference between an umbrella and a pure excess liability policy?",o:["There's no difference","An excess policy follows the underlying policy's terms. An umbrella may also broaden coverage","Excess covers property","Umbrella is only personal"],a:1,e:"Excess policies 'follow form', adding limits only. Umbrellas add limits and can fill some gaps."},
{d:"com",q:"A client's umbrella requires $2,000,000 of underlying CGL coverage, but the client lets the CGL lapse. A $3,000,000 claim occurs. What is the likely result?",o:["The umbrella pays the full $3,000,000","The umbrella pays only what it would have paid above the required underlying limit, leaving a large gap","RIBO pays the gap","The claim is void"],a:1,e:"Umbrellas require the underlying policies to stay in force. If they lapse, the insured is responsible for the gap the primary policy would have covered."},
{d:"com",q:"An accountant's claims-made policy has a retroactive date of 2018. The accountant switches insurers in 2026. What should the broker try to do?",o:["Accept a new retroactive date of 2026","Keep the 2018 retroactive date with the new insurer (prior acts coverage)","Cancel coverage","Change to occurrence CGL"],a:1,e:"Keeping the original retroactive date protects past work. A new 2026 retro date would leave claims about work from 2018 to 2026 uncovered."},
{d:"com",q:"Under a claims-made professional liability policy, a client says something went wrong last month but hasn't made a formal claim. What should the professional usually do?",o:["Wait for the lawsuit","Report the circumstance to the insurer now, as the policy allows","Cancel the policy","Pay the client privately"],a:1,e:"Many claims-made policies let you report circumstances that may lead to a claim, so the claim is tied to the current policy."},
{d:"com",q:"A claims-made policy includes defence costs within the limit. What does that mean?",o:["Legal costs are paid in addition to the limit","Legal costs reduce the amount left to pay damages","There's no defence coverage","Defence is paid by RIBO"],a:1,e:"When defence costs are 'within limits', every dollar spent on lawyers reduces the limit available for settlement."},
{d:"com",q:"An engineering firm designs a building, and the design flaw causes a collapse years later. Which coverage is meant for design errors?",o:["Professional liability (E&O)","CGL only","Builders' risk","Crime"],a:0,e:"Errors in professional services, such as design, are professional liability exposures. CGLs usually exclude them."},
{d:"com",q:"Who does a D&O policy protect?",o:["Customers who slip on the premises","Directors and officers personally, and often the organization, for wrongful acts in management","Employees injured at work","The company's vehicles"],a:1,e:"D&O protects individuals in leadership roles against claims for wrongful acts, such as breach of duty or mismanagement."},
{d:"com",q:"A former employee sues a company for wrongful dismissal and discrimination. Which coverage is designed for this?",o:["Employment practices liability (EPL)","CGL Coverage A","Crime","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Employment practices liability covers claims such as wrongful dismissal, discrimination and harassment. CGLs exclude them."},
{d:"com",q:"A ransomware attack locks a retailer's computer systems for five days and it can't sell online. Which coverage pays the lost income?",o:["Cyber business interruption (first-party cyber)","Standard BI on the property policy","CGL Coverage A","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Standard BI usually needs physical damage. Cyber policies include network interruption coverage for income lost due to an attack."},
{d:"com",q:"Customers sue a clinic after their health records were exposed in a data breach. Which coverage responds to their claims?",o:["First-party cyber","Third-party cyber liability (privacy liability)","Crime","Equipment breakdown"],a:1,e:"Third-party cyber covers claims by others whose data was exposed, plus related regulatory proceedings where insurable."},
{d:"com",q:"Which question is MOST relevant when underwriting a cyber policy?",o:["Does the business use multi-factor authentication and keep backups?","How many parking spaces are there?","Is the roof less than 20 years old?","Does the owner smoke?"],a:0,e:"Cyber underwriting focuses on security controls such as multi-factor authentication, backups, patching and staff training."},
{d:"com",q:"A contractor must provide a certificate of insurance to a general contractor before starting work. What does the certificate prove?",o:["That the policy was in force on the date issued, with the coverages and limits shown","That every claim will be paid","That the general contractor is insured","That the premium is paid for 10 years"],a:0,e:"A certificate shows coverage as of its issue date. It doesn't amend the policy, and coverage can change after it's issued."},
{d:"com",q:"A broker is asked to issue a certificate saying a policy covers something it doesn't. What should the broker do?",o:["Issue it to keep the client happy","Refuse. Certificates must accurately reflect the policy","Issue it with a small disclaimer","Ask RIBO for permission"],a:1,e:"Issuing a misleading certificate is misconduct and an E&O risk. The broker must arrange the coverage properly or decline."},
{d:"com",q:"A general contractor wants to be added as an additional insured on a subcontractor's CGL. What does that give the general contractor?",o:["Coverage under the subcontractor's CGL for liability arising from the subcontractor's work, as the endorsement defines","Ownership of the policy","Property coverage for the building","Nothing"],a:0,e:"Additional insured status extends the named insured's liability coverage to another party, within the limits of the endorsement."},
{d:"com",q:"What is a 'waiver of subrogation' that a client is asked to sign in a contract?",o:["An agreement that the insurer won't recover from the other contract party after paying a loss","A claims form","A refusal to buy insurance","A RIBO document"],a:0,e:"A waiver of subrogation gives up the insurer's right to sue the other party. The insured should check the policy allows it before signing."},
{d:"com",q:"A business buys a builders' risk policy for a new warehouse. Which is typically covered?",o:["The building and materials during construction, including materials on site","The contractor's own tools","Bodily injury to the public","The finished building after it's occupied"],a:0,e:"Builders' risk covers the structure and materials being built. Tools need an equipment floater, and liability needs a CGL."},
{d:"com",q:"Why is theft of building materials a concern on builders' risk policies?",o:["Materials can't be insured","Unoccupied construction sites are targets, so insurers may require fencing, security and limits on theft","Only lumber is covered","Theft is covered by the CGL"],a:1,e:"Construction sites have high theft risk. Insurers often set theft conditions, deductibles or security requirements."},
{d:"com",q:"A landscaping company's trailer of mowers is stolen from a job site. Which coverage fits best?",o:["Contractor's equipment floater","Building coverage","Builders' risk","CGL Coverage A"],a:0,e:"Floaters cover mobile equipment wherever it is, including at job sites and in transit."},
{d:"com",q:"A business ships goods to a customer in Quebec by a hired courier. The goods are destroyed in a truck fire. Who usually needs which coverage?",o:["The owner of the goods needs transit or cargo coverage, and the carrier needs motor truck cargo for its liability","No one needs coverage","Only the customer's home insurance applies","The CGL covers the goods"],a:0,e:"Owners insure their goods in transit. Carriers insure their legal liability for goods they haul. Carrier liability can be limited, so owners shouldn't rely on it alone."},
{d:"com",q:"A trucking company's motor truck cargo policy covers which risk?",o:["The trucking company's legal liability for the customer's goods it carries","The trucks themselves","The drivers' injuries","The customer's warehouse"],a:0,e:"Motor truck cargo covers the carrier's liability for goods in its care, up to the policy limit and conditions."},
{d:"com",q:"What does a 'bill of lading' show in a shipment?",o:["The terms of the shipment, the goods and the parties","The insurance premium","The driver's record","The CGL limit"],a:0,e:"A bill of lading is the contract and receipt for goods shipped. It matters for deciding who bears the risk of loss."},
{d:"com",q:"A labour and material payment bond protects whom?",o:["Subcontractors and suppliers, by guaranteeing they'll be paid","The contractor's employees against injury","The public against accidents","The contractor against theft"],a:0,e:"Payment bonds guarantee that the principal will pay its subcontractors and suppliers."},
{d:"com",q:"A contractor bids on a city project and must guarantee that, if it wins, it will sign the contract and provide the required performance bond. Which bond is this?",o:["Bid bond","Fidelity bond","Payment bond","License bond"],a:0,e:"A bid bond guarantees the winning bidder will enter into the contract and supply the required bonds."},
{d:"com",q:"If a surety pays an obligee because the contractor defaulted, what can the surety do next?",o:["Nothing","Recover the money from the contractor (principal), usually under an indemnity agreement","Raise the obligee's premium","Cancel the city's insurance"],a:1,e:"Suretyship assumes the principal will repay any loss. Sureties take indemnity agreements, often personal ones from owners."},
{d:"com",q:"How is a surety bond different from an insurance policy?",o:["It isn't different","The surety expects no losses and can recover any payment from the principal. Insurance expects some losses and pools them","Bonds cover property","Bonds are regulated by RIBO only"],a:1,e:"Surety is credit-based: the surety lends its guarantee. Insurance transfers and pools risk."},
{d:"com",q:"A large shopping centre is insured under a subscription policy with three insurers. One insurer goes bankrupt. Who pays its share of a loss?",o:["The other two insurers","No one under that policy. Each subscriber is liable only for its own share","RIBO","The broker"],a:1,e:"Subscribers aren't jointly liable. The failed insurer's share must be pursued through its insolvency process."},
{d:"com",q:"In a subscription policy, what does the 'lead insurer' usually do?",o:["Pays all claims alone","Sets terms and often handles administration or claims for the group","Regulates the others","Owns the property"],a:1,e:"The lead insurer usually negotiates wording and coordinates. Each subscriber still pays only its share."},
{d:"com",q:"A farmer needs coverage for their house, barns, livestock, machinery and farm liability. What is usually used?",o:["A farm package policy","A homeowners policy","A CGL only","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Farm policies combine dwelling, farm buildings, equipment, livestock and farm liability in one package."},
{d:"com",q:"A small consulting business runs from the owner's home office with no clients visiting. The owner asks if a commercial package policy is needed. A broker would MOST likely first consider:",o:["A home-based business endorsement or small business policy, depending on the exposures","Builders' risk","Marine cargo","OAP 4"],a:0,e:"Small, low-risk home businesses can often be covered by an endorsement. More exposures, such as clients visiting or stock, need a business policy."},
{d:"com",q:"A business has several commercial vehicles and wants new vehicles covered automatically as they're bought, with periodic reporting. Which approach fits?",o:["A fleet policy with blanket or automatic coverage for added vehicles","Separate personal policies","A CGL","SPF 6"],a:0,e:"Fleet policies can cover newly acquired vehicles automatically, with reporting and premium adjustment."},
{d:"com",q:"A contractor's pickup truck is used mainly for business and registered to the business. It is usually insured on:",o:["The owner's personal homeowners policy","An OAP 1 written as a commercial or individually rated commercial vehicle","A CGL","SPF 6"],a:1,e:"Business-owned vehicles are insured on the OAP 1 (individually rated commercial auto) or a fleet policy. The CGL excludes owned autos."},
{d:"com",q:"A delivery company's drivers have several speeding tickets and one at-fault accident. How does this affect the commercial auto policy?",o:["It doesn't","It can raise premiums or cause insurers to exclude or decline drivers","It only affects the CGL","It lowers the deductible"],a:1,e:"Driver records drive commercial auto rating and eligibility. Insurers may exclude drivers who don't meet their standards."},
{d:"com",q:"An auto dealership lets customers test drive cars from its lot. Which policy is designed for this exposure?",o:["OAP 4 garage policy","Homeowners","SPF 6","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"The garage policy covers dealer operations, including vehicles driven by customers and employees on test drives."},
{d:"com",q:"A car repair shop damages a customer's car while it's on the hoist. Which garage policy coverage responds?",o:["Customers' automobiles in the insured's care, custody or control","The customer's own DCPD","CGL Coverage B","Crime"],a:0,e:"Garage policies include coverage for customers' vehicles in the garage's care, custody or control."},
{d:"com",q:"Why is CVOR relevant to an insurance broker?",o:["Insurers check CVOR status and safety record when underwriting trucking risks","It replaces insurance","It's a RIBO licence","It sets CGL limits"],a:0,e:"CVOR (Commercial Vehicle Operator's Registration) records show a carrier's safety and compliance history, which insurers review."},
{d:"com",q:"An employee uses the company's rented car to visit a client and causes an accident. The rental company's insurance is minimal. What should the employer have?",o:["OPCF 27B for rented vehicles and non-owned auto liability","Only a CGL","Only crime coverage","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Businesses renting vehicles need protection for damage to the rented vehicle (OPCF 27B) and liability, such as non-owned auto coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A business moves goods between its warehouses in its own trucks. The goods are damaged in an upset. Which coverage protects the business's goods?",o:["Motor truck cargo (owner's form) or transit coverage","CGL Coverage A","D&O","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Owner's cargo or transit coverage protects the business's own goods in its own vehicles."},
{d:"com",q:"A pharmaceutical warehouse has temperature-sensitive stock. Which coverage protects against stock spoiling when refrigeration fails from a breakdown?",o:["Equipment breakdown with spoilage or refrigeration coverage","CGL","Crime","Fidelity bond"],a:0,e:"Spoilage coverage, often under equipment breakdown, pays for perishable stock lost because refrigeration fails."},
{d:"com",q:"What does a 'deductible' typically look like on a commercial liability policy?",o:["Liability policies never have deductibles","Some liability policies have a deductible per claim, often including defence costs","It's always 50% of the loss","It applies only to property"],a:1,e:"Commercial liability policies can carry per-claim deductibles. How defence costs count depends on the wording."},
{d:"com",q:"A large business keeps the first $250,000 of each liability claim itself before its insurance responds. This is called:",o:["A self-insured retention (SIR)","Co-insurance","An aggregate","Subrogation"],a:0,e:"A self-insured retention is an amount the insured pays and often handles itself before the policy responds."},
{d:"com",q:"A professional liability policy is written on a claims-made basis. A claim is made after the policy period ends, but it's for work done during the policy. If there's no extended reporting period, the claim is:",o:["Covered","Not covered under that policy","Covered at half the limit","Covered by the CGL"],a:1,e:"Claims-made policies respond to claims made during the policy period, or during an extended reporting period if one applies."},
{d:"com",q:"What does 'trade credit insurance' protect?",o:["A business's accounts receivable if customers don't pay because of insolvency or default","Employee theft","Goods at sea","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Trade credit protects receivables, helping businesses offer credit terms and get financing."},
{d:"com",q:"An importer brings electronics by ocean freight from Asia to Toronto. Which coverage protects the goods during the voyage?",o:["Ocean marine cargo","Motor truck cargo only","CGL","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Marine cargo insures goods in transit by sea, often warehouse to warehouse including land legs."},
{d:"com",q:"Which business is MOST likely to need professional liability (E&O) coverage?",o:["A real estate agent giving advice to clients","A parking lot owner","A warehouse with no services","A farm"],a:0,e:"Anyone giving professional advice or services, like real estate agents, consultants, accountants and brokers, has E&O exposure."},
{d:"com",q:"A non-profit's volunteer treasurer is sued for mismanaging funds. Which coverage protects the treasurer?",o:["D&O liability for the non-profit","Crime","CGL Coverage A","Equipment breakdown"],a:0,e:"Non-profit D&O covers directors and officers, including volunteers, for management decisions."},
{d:"com",q:"A sporting goods store sells a defective bicycle helmet that fails and a customer is injured. Which part of the store's CGL responds?",o:["Products-completed operations","Tenants' legal liability","Crime","Business interruption"],a:0,e:"Injury from products the insured sold, after they've left the premises, falls under products-completed operations."},
{d:"com",q:"A broker places a small restaurant's property, liability and crime coverages together in one policy. This is called:",o:["A commercial package policy","A subscription policy","A surety bond","An excess policy"],a:0,e:"Package policies combine property, liability and other coverages for small and mid-size businesses, often at lower cost."},
{d:"com",q:"A tenant in a strip mall signs a lease requiring it to insure the building's plate glass windows. Which coverage would handle this?",o:["Glass coverage under its property policy or a glass endorsement","CGL Coverage B","D&O","OAP 4"],a:0,e:"Leases often shift glass responsibility to tenants. Glass coverage pays for breakage of insured glass."},
{d:"com",q:"What is the purpose of a 'loss payee' or mortgagee clause on a commercial property policy?",o:["To protect the lender's financial interest in the insured property","To lower the deductible","To add the landlord as an additional insured for liability","To cover employee theft"],a:0,e:"The lender is named so claim payments protect its interest in the property it financed."},
{d:"com",q:"A business moves to a new location but forgets to tell its broker. A fire happens at the new location. What's the main risk?",o:["None","The policy may not cover the new location because it isn't a described premises","The deductible doubles","The CGL increases"],a:1,e:"Property policies cover described locations. New locations should be reported right away, though some policies give limited automatic coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A client asks why its commercial property premium went up although it had no claims. Which is a valid reason?",o:["Rising construction costs, higher building values and industry-wide losses can raise premiums","Brokers raise premiums for commission","RIBO sets property rates","Premiums only change after claims"],a:0,e:"Inflation, rebuild costs, catastrophe losses and market conditions affect premiums even for claim-free clients."},
{d:"com",q:"A client wants the cheapest commercial policy, and the broker notices it excludes sewer backup. What should the broker do?",o:["Say nothing","Explain the gap, offer options, and document the client's decision","Add it without asking","Cancel the quote"],a:1,e:"Brokers must explain significant gaps so the client can make an informed choice, and keep notes of the decision."},
{d:"com",q:"A business owner says, 'I don't need liability insurance because I'm incorporated.' The BEST response is:",o:["That's correct","The corporation itself can still be sued and lose its assets, and some claims can reach individuals","Corporations can't be sued","Only sole proprietors need insurance"],a:1,e:"Incorporation limits shareholders' personal liability, but the corporation's own assets are exposed, and directors and officers can face personal claims."},
// SET 3 — TRAVEL
{d:"trv",q:"A client is going to Florida for 10 days. They're healthy, with no medications. Which product is MOST suitable?",o:["Single-trip emergency medical","Visitors to Canada","Trip cancellation only","Builders' risk"],a:0,e:"Single-trip emergency medical fits one trip for a resident with provincial coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"What does 'emergency medical' travel insurance generally NOT cover?",o:["Sudden illness abroad","Elective or non-emergency treatment the traveller chose to get abroad","An injury in a car accident abroad","Emergency hospital stays"],a:1,e:"Travel medical covers emergencies, not planned or elective treatment."},
{d:"trv",q:"A traveller is stabilized after an emergency abroad. The insurer may require them to:",o:["Stay abroad indefinitely","Return to Canada for further treatment when medically able, or coverage may end","Pay OHIP","Cancel the policy"],a:1,e:"Policies cover the emergency. Once stable, the insurer may arrange return home, and refusing can limit coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"What is 'medical evacuation' or repatriation coverage?",o:["Coverage for the cost of transporting the traveller home or to a suitable facility for medical reasons","Trip cancellation","Baggage","Life insurance"],a:0,e:"Medical evacuation can cost tens of thousands of dollars, so it's a key part of travel medical coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client's luggage is lost by the airline. Which coverage helps?",o:["Baggage coverage, often after the airline's own compensation","Emergency medical","Trip interruption","OPCF 20"],a:0,e:"Baggage coverage responds to lost or damaged luggage, often excess of the carrier's liability."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client's flight is delayed, and they miss a prepaid cruise departure. Which coverage MAY help?",o:["Trip interruption or missed connection coverage, depending on the policy","Emergency medical","Coverage E","CGL"],a:0,e:"Some trip interruption policies cover missed connections or the costs of catching up to a trip."},
{d:"trv",q:"A traveller didn't disclose a heart condition on the medical questionnaire. They have a heart attack abroad. What's the MOST likely outcome?",o:["Fully paid","The claim may be denied or the policy voided because of the misrepresentation","OHIP pays everything","The broker pays"],a:1,e:"Accurate questionnaires are essential. Misrepresentation can void coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client asks whether to buy trip cancellation right after booking or right before leaving. What's the BEST advice?",o:["Right before leaving","Soon after booking, because cancellation coverage protects only against reasons that arise after it's bought","Never","Only if the trip is under $500"],a:1,e:"Buying cancellation coverage early protects against covered events that happen before departure."},
{d:"trv",q:"What does a travel policy's 'stability period' refer to?",o:["How long the trip lasts","How long a pre-existing condition must be stable before departure for it to be covered","The deductible","The policy term"],a:1,e:"Stability periods, often 90 or 180 days, determine whether pre-existing conditions are covered."},
{d:"trv",q:"Which change would generally make a medical condition 'unstable' under a travel policy?",o:["Taking the same medication at the same dose","A new prescription or a change in dose","A routine check-up with no changes","Buying a new suitcase"],a:1,e:"New medications, changed doses, new symptoms or new treatment usually count as instability. Check the definitions."},
{d:"trv",q:"A traveller over a certain age is asked to complete a medical questionnaire. Why?",o:["It's a formality","Age and health status affect the risk and the premium","RIBO requires it","For marketing"],a:1,e:"Travel insurers rate by age and health. Questionnaires set eligibility and price."},
{d:"trv",q:"What should a broker tell a client about travelling while pregnant?",o:["Nothing","Policies may limit coverage for pregnancy-related issues near the due date, so check the wording","It's always fully covered","It's never covered"],a:1,e:"Many policies limit pregnancy coverage in the weeks around the due date."},
{d:"trv",q:"A traveller injures themselves while intoxicated abroad. Under many policies:",o:["Always covered","Claims related to alcohol or drug abuse may be excluded","Only covered by OHIP","Double benefits"],a:1,e:"Alcohol and drug related exclusions are common in travel medical policies."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client holding annual multi-trip coverage takes a 40-day trip, but the plan covers trips up to 30 days. What's the result?",o:["The whole trip is covered","Days beyond 30 may not be covered unless a top-up is bought","The policy is cancelled","OHIP covers the extra days"],a:1,e:"Multi-trip plans have per-trip limits. Longer trips need top-up coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client wants to extend their trip while abroad. What should they do?",o:["Nothing","Contact the insurer before the original coverage ends to request an extension, which may depend on their health","Buy a new policy after it expires","Ask OHIP"],a:1,e:"Extensions must usually be arranged before expiry. A claim or health change can affect eligibility."},
{d:"trv",q:"Why might a broker recommend that clients carry their policy number and assistance phone number while travelling?",o:["For marketing","So they can contact the assistance company immediately in an emergency, as the policy requires","It's required by law","To get discounts"],a:1,e:"Quick contact with the assistance company is often a condition of coverage."},
{d:"trv",q:"Which statement about travel insurance claims is correct?",o:["Keep no receipts","Keep receipts, medical reports and documents to support the claim","Claims are paid automatically","Only the broker can claim"],a:1,e:"Documentation supports claims. Missing paperwork can delay or reduce payment."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client asks if travel medical covers a routine prescription refill they forgot to bring. Generally:",o:["Yes, always","No. Routine or non-emergency costs usually aren't covered","Only under trip cancellation","Only under baggage"],a:1,e:"Travel medical covers emergencies, not routine care or forgotten medications."},
{d:"trv",q:"A client has a 'deductible' on their travel medical policy. What does it do?",o:["Increases benefits","The client pays the first part of each claim, which usually lowers the premium","Removes coverage","Is paid by OHIP"],a:1,e:"Travel deductibles work like other deductibles: they share the loss and reduce premium."},
{d:"trv",q:"An international student coming to study in Ontario has no OHIP coverage. What should they look at?",o:["Trip cancellation","Health coverage for students or visitors, such as a university plan or visitors to Canada coverage","OAP 1","CGL"],a:1,e:"Students without provincial coverage need a university health plan or private visitor or student coverage."},
{d:"com",q:"A store owner wants protection if a trusted cashier secretly steals cash over several months. Which coverage fits?",o:["A 3-D policy (dishonesty, disappearance and destruction)","A burglary policy","A hold-up (robbery) policy","A theft policy that excludes employees"],a:0,e:"Theft by your own employees is covered by employee dishonesty, which comes in a 3-D policy or a fidelity bond. Burglary, robbery and theft coverages are for outsiders and exclude employees."}
];
